    <node id="&quot;NUCLEUS HIVE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A small colony of bees used to start a new hive, recommended as one of the primary options for beginners."&lt;SEP&gt;"A small starter colony of bees, also known as a nuc, used by beekeepers to begin a new colony."&lt;SEP&gt;"Also known as a 'nuc', it is a small starter colony of bees on a few frames, used to establish a new hive."&lt;SEP&gt;"Also known as a nuc, this is a small, complete functioning colony with frames, comb, brood, and a queen, often purchased as a starter unit."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-18646221e86ea90ee41e4d859e5ef7b1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-759e701020a9c2cf1b1fc48ccd55df97&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a9465e78f869f57a714827f88ed9c132&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f6b1093c60c117041d669d3950218419</data>
      <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 31}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 415}, {"level": 2, "cluster": 1593}]</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PACKAGE BEES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A collection of bees sold in a box without a comb, used by novice beekeepers to start a colony due to their availability and low defensiveness."&lt;SEP&gt;"A method of selling bees where a specific quantity of bees is packaged and shipped to the buyer, typically in the spring."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bees sold in a package, serving as a starting point for novice beekeepers to establish a colony."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-18646221e86ea90ee41e4d859e5ef7b1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-759e701020a9c2cf1b1fc48ccd55df97&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d621a8bb4b7eb07600bbbfbdafcad0b</data>
      <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 31}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 415}, {"level": 2, "cluster": 1593}]</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;LOCAL BEEKEEPERS' ORGANIZATION&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Community groups that provide support, information, and resources for beekeepers, including beginners."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-759e701020a9c2cf1b1fc48ccd55df97</data>
      <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 4}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 106}]</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;COUNTY EXTENSION OFFICE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A local government office that provides educational resources and information, including directions to beekeeping associations."&lt;SEP&gt;"A local office that provides nutrient and soil testing services for farmers."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-43b1ee12a99c9b1b3ebd9a36f25d2a6f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-759e701020a9c2cf1b1fc48ccd55df97</data>
      <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 4}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 106}]</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;HONEY BEE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"NORMAL_ENTITY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A specific bee known scientifically as Apis mellifera, also called the Western or European honey bee."&lt;SEP&gt;"A specific type of bee that beekeepers manage; there are only six species worldwide."&lt;SEP&gt;"An insect central to pollination and beekeeping, described as having healing power and currently under profound ecological stress."&lt;SEP&gt;"An insect crucial for the pollination of almond trees, capable of forming large, organized, and portable communities."&lt;SEP&gt;"Insects characterized by a caste system (queen, worker, drone) and complex social behaviors driven by physiological development and stimuli."&lt;SEP&gt;"The bees responsible for maintaining the hive and defending it against invaders like wax moths."&lt;SEP&gt;"The honey bee is a pollinating insect known for its social structure in colonies and its instinct to gather and store nectar as honey."&lt;SEP&gt;"The honey bee is a social insect that forms colonies acting as superorganisms, consisting of queens, workers, and drones."&lt;SEP&gt;"The honey bee is the central biological subject, characterized by its social colony structure, specific dietary needs (nectar and pollen), and behaviors such as swarming."&lt;SEP&gt;"The honey bee is the species of bee managed by beekeepers for honey production and pollination."&lt;SEP&gt;"The host insect for tracheal mites, characterized by specific population cycles and colony behaviors."&lt;SEP&gt;"The primary insect species being managed, which can exhibit different behaviors based on stock (European vs. African) and hygiene levels."&lt;SEP&gt;"The species of bee described as a valuable pollinator that exhibits constancy in its foraging habits."</data>
