    <node id="&quot;PARTHENOGENESIS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"SOCIAL SCIENCES"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Parthenogenesis is a biological process where normal queens develop from unfertilized eggs, noted as being rare in European honey bees but more common in Cape bees."&lt;SEP&gt;"Parthenogenesis, or virgin birth, is the process by which unfertilized eggs develop into drones, resulting in offspring with a grandfather but no father."&lt;SEP&gt;"The biological process by which unfertilized bee eggs develop into queens."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9654edf0284987ba4f0c8bde4acffe9b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b9bf03b457112a6e1094db904efcfe2d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-82e31ea2adf95c6c787a2e0a6348bae1</data>
      <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 31}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 415}, {"level": 2, "cluster": 1592}, {"level": 3, "cluster": 2082}]</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;SPERMATHECA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"NORMAL_ENTITY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A specialized organ used by the queen bee to store sperm for the purpose of fertilizing eggs throughout her life."&lt;SEP&gt;"An organ in the queen bee that stores millions of sperm received during mating flights."&lt;SEP&gt;"The spermatheca is the specialized organ in the queen bee's body used to store sperm received during mating flights."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b9bf03b457112a6e1094db904efcfe2d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bed6fe8d6b1e0689fd3080d94a55bcac&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6550c1217ac242a6cb1c3d9a5de5ee35</data>
      <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 31}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 415}, {"level": 2, "cluster": 1592}, {"level": 3, "cluster": 2084}]</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;HOUSE BEE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"NORMAL_ENTITY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A bee that performs internal hive duties such as brood rearing and wax secretion before graduating to a field bee."&lt;SEP&gt;"A house bee is a developmental stage of a worker bee's life following the brood stage."&lt;SEP&gt;"A phase in a worker bee's life where she performs internal hive duties such as cleaning and nursing brood."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bees that remain within the hive to process nectar into honey and communicate the quality of incoming resources to foragers."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6f51ec44f0c17ad5253b21395cf12591&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-65de87b95deadb72c4827d5a3bd2f316&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b9bf03b457112a6e1094db904efcfe2d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-78182a0a75dac1ecf9d9f1cc16f3a0ff</data>
      <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 31}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 412}, {"level": 2, "cluster": 1579}]</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;FIELD BEE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"NORMAL_ENTITY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A bee that has graduated from house duties to spend the remainder of its life foraging for nectar, pollen, water, or propolis."&lt;SEP&gt;"A field bee is the final developmental stage of a worker bee, involving foraging and outside labor."&lt;SEP&gt;"A phase in a worker bee's life where she performs external duties, primarily foraging for nectar and pollen."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bees that leave the hive to gather resources like nectar and pollen from the environment."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6f51ec44f0c17ad5253b21395cf12591&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-65de87b95deadb72c4827d5a3bd2f316&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b9bf03b457112a6e1094db904efcfe2d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-78182a0a75dac1ecf9d9f1cc16f3a0ff</data>
      <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 31}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 412}, {"level": 2, "cluster": 1579}]</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;NURSE BEES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"NORMAL_ENTITY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Nurse bees are worker bees that secrete royal jelly to feed young larvae."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-b9bf03b457112a6e1094db904efcfe2d</data>
      <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 31}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 412}, {"level": 2, "cluster": 1580}, {"level": 3, "cluster": 2061}]</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;WORKER BEE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"NORMAL_ENTITY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A caste of honey bee that performs various colony tasks including cleaning, feeding larvae, building comb, and foraging."&lt;SEP&gt;"Bees responsible for tasks such as feeding brood, cell capping, foraging for nectar, and guarding the hive."&lt;SEP&gt;"The most populous member of the colony, responsible for all hive maintenance and external tasks, with roles that evolve based on age, including nursing, cleaning, and foraging."&lt;SEP&gt;"The worker bee is the primary life force of the colony, performing a wide variety of tasks including brood rearing, foraging, and honey production based on age and colony needs."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6f51ec44f0c17ad5253b21395cf12591&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-14c389aa55b6d5b4c773f2c917b36e7a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-78182a0a75dac1ecf9d9f1cc16f3a0ff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-43e80ed5175459c22df05858f37cc067</data>
      <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 31}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 412}, {"level": 2, "cluster": 1580}, {"level": 3, "cluster": 2062}]</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;NURSE BEE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"NORMAL_ENTITY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Specialized worker bees that produce royal jelly to feed the larvae."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-14c389aa55b6d5b4c773f2c917b36e7a</data>
      <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 31}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 412}, {"level": 2, "cluster": 1580}, {"level": 3, "cluster": 2061}]</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;QUEEN BEE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"NORMAL_ENTITY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The largest member of the bee colony, acting as the reproductive organ of the hive, laying up to 2,000 eggs per day and producing pheromones to communicate hive health."&lt;SEP&gt;"The queen bee is the primary reproductive female in a colony, responsible for laying eggs and maintaining the hive's social structure."&lt;SEP&gt;"The reproductive female of the colony, responsible for laying eggs; she requires a specialized diet and larger cell for development."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-14c389aa55b6d5b4c773f2c917b36e7a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-99f3cfb348742cf36dd48849fb6973f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-43e80ed5175459c22df05858f37cc067</data>
      <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 31}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 412}, {"level": 2, "cluster": 1580}, {"level": 3, "cluster": 2062}]</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;DRONE BEE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"NORMAL_ENTITY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"The male bee whose primary function is to mate with a queen; characterized by a longer brood stage and a non-contributory role in hive labor."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-14c389aa55b6d5b4c773f2c917b36e7a</data>
      <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 31}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 412}, {"level": 2, "cluster": 1580}, {"level": 3, "cluster": 2062}]</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;HIVE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PRODUCT"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A hive is the structure that houses a bee colony and provides the environment for them to raise brood and store honey."&lt;SEP&gt;"A hive is the structure used to house honey bee colonies, consisting of various boxes, supers, and frames."&lt;SEP&gt;"A home for bees, typically human-manufactured consisting of a bottom board, covers, and stacked boxes with frames."&lt;SEP&gt;"A structure used to house bees, consisting of frames and covers, used during the installation process of a bee package."&lt;SEP&gt;"A structured enclosure used to house bee colonies, consisting of frames, foundations, and covers."&lt;SEP&gt;"The artificial structure used to house a colony of bees and facilitate beekeeping management."&lt;SEP&gt;"The hive is the physical structure housing the bee colony, consisting of components like the outer cover, inner cover, and frames."&lt;SEP&gt;"The hive is the structure housing the bee colony, used for monitoring weight and inspecting bee activity."&lt;SEP&gt;"The hive is the structure or housing where the bee colony resides, consisting of bodies, frames, and covers."&lt;SEP&gt;"The hive is the structure used to house bee colonies, consisting of components like the bottom board, hive bodies, and frames."&lt;SEP&gt;"The physical structure and environment where the honey bee colony resides, contains cells for eggs, larvae, and storage of nectar and pollen."&lt;SEP&gt;"The structure housing a bee colony, consisting of frames, combs, and supers."&lt;SEP&gt;"The structure that houses a bee colony, consisting of frames, hive bodies, and covers."&lt;SEP&gt;"The structure used to house honeybee colonies, consisting of components like the bottom board, hive bodies, and inner cover."&lt;SEP&gt;"The structure where the bee colony resides, containing waxen cells, the brood nest, and serving as the center for processing nectar and honey."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3646f7feb4b27315450c6eb80594e753&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-43e80ed5175459c22df05858f37cc067&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67600081c317292f8ef362e2f5621f34&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6706ffd7485284387c77b1f38a66ce9c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c516e184f72cae24cee188c702d41f00&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-caeb5d2bd49a72e7116025708c05e9f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0f4bb80b935e118383082352a101ac3c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f6b1093c60c117041d669d3950218419&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3c3e0c4c99d600e81552689af79afdcd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-14c389aa55b6d5b4c773f2c917b36e7a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b30dd2b72067de8dc8358baf75e16d21&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f3a9f95df2c28cf16cc0339d5b436227&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b902e3da084c9ce16ad8c497c00c21e2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4ff7d8f22cb1bb72cdb48f20d4975e8d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0385df8773cab416fd9b294da95cf91d</data>
      <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 31}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 413}, {"level": 2, "cluster": 1585}, {"level": 3, "cluster": 2071}]</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;COLONY&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A collective group of honey bees acting as a single biological unit with a population cycle involving growth and decline."&lt;SEP&gt;"A social group of bees that works together for survival, brood rearing, and population growth."&lt;SEP&gt;"The colony is described as a superorganism, representing the collective organized structure of the bees acting as a single entity."&lt;SEP&gt;"The colony is the social organizational unit of bees, functioning as a collective to ensure survival through divided labor and specific roles."&lt;SEP&gt;"The social unit of honey bees working together for mutual success and survival through a division of labor."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-78182a0a75dac1ecf9d9f1cc16f3a0ff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-651bdc43ce4f434b1e5c689b3528db55&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d689db19e9533ba99ddacc008292886b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-349ce4585e93cae4853e6da2e815c494&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-14c389aa55b6d5b4c773f2c917b36e7a</data>
      <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 31}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 413}, {"level": 2, "cluster": 1583}, {"level": 3, "cluster": 2067}]</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;PROPOLIS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"NORMAL_ENTITY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A potent naturally occurring antimicrobial material produced by bees to seal hive surfaces and maintain a sterile environment."&lt;SEP&gt;"A resinous material collected from tree bark and buds used by bees to strengthen the comb and seal cracks in the hive."&lt;SEP&gt;"A resinous mixture produced by bees, used in this context to embalm a dead mouse within the hive."&lt;SEP&gt;"A resinous mixture produced by honey bees that can glue the outer cover of a hive."&lt;SEP&gt;"A resinous mixture produced by honey bees, noted for its healing properties and potential use as a varnish."&lt;SEP&gt;"A resinous mixture produced by honey bees, used by bees to seal the hive and by humans as a varnish or for health purposes."&lt;SEP&gt;"A resinous mixture produced by honey bees, which in this context is used to fill the slits in traps."&lt;SEP&gt;"A resinous substance collected by bees from tree bark and buds, used for hive sealing and as a natural health aid in products like toothpaste."&lt;SEP&gt;"A resinous substance collected by bees from tree bark and buds, used for reinforcing combs and sealing gaps in the hive."&lt;SEP&gt;"A resinous substance collected by bees from tree bark and buds, used to seal cracks and crevices in the hive."&lt;SEP&gt;"A sticky antimicrobial substance made of plant resins used by bees as building material and varnish."&lt;SEP&gt;"A sticky, resinous substance collected by bees to seal and reinforce their hives."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-7f35b518c8481093038a38bf2b64387d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0bfa52bca196a2a92c24daffb2347956&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5cdc449839e793310ef8f89b87479c09&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b71c661e88d96156e87449e9332b56ce&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9654edf0284987ba4f0c8bde4acffe9b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6550c1217ac242a6cb1c3d9a5de5ee35&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c29c8fc62f2aad355ff0f1407fba86a8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-14c389aa55b6d5b4c773f2c917b36e7a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b652e467f32b1db6fbb1cf5b9315586e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2c1514064bbd0a8e145cda20ebb9ecab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6f9390615c59b7685e5899983f69d846&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-58530be97ce8bec48257c2ee46f928b4</data>
      <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 31}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 408}, {"level": 2, "cluster": 1561}, {"level": 3, "cluster": 2051}]</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;HONEY BEES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"NORMAL_ENTITY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Honey bees are insects that thrive on wholesome natural foods and are the subject of the beekeeping techniques described, including feeding and hive management."&lt;SEP&gt;"Honey bees are the insects being housed and cared for in the hives, requiring specific nourishment like honey and pollen for health."&lt;SEP&gt;"Honey bees are the primary biological subject of the text, known for their social organization, communication via pheromones, and reaction to smoke."&lt;SEP&gt;"Honey bees are the primary subject of the text, characterized by complex social behaviors and roles within a colony, including workers, drones, and queens."&lt;SEP&gt;"The insect species subject to various diseases and responsible for producing pollen, propolis, royal jelly, and venom."&lt;SEP&gt;"The primary subject of the text, insects whose behavior is governed by physiological development, external stimuli, and pheromones."&lt;SEP&gt;"The species of bees managed for honey production and pollination."&lt;SEP&gt;"The species of bees used in beekeeping, known for foraging within a 1 to 2 mile radius."&lt;SEP&gt;"The species of insects that form colonies, engage in brood rearing, and collect nectar and pollen."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-78182a0a75dac1ecf9d9f1cc16f3a0ff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-651bdc43ce4f434b1e5c689b3528db55&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-63d47730064116b6f4fe93ddb307901d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c6739d4c7c92b4bbf50b18187fe06b72&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b71c661e88d96156e87449e9332b56ce&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c516e184f72cae24cee188c702d41f00&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee2dbfbb5c1a5664034bad31b4143c62&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8e06b0074dff6e9083043d922831e71f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a230cf3f43e673f8797237bc5d97548</data>
      <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 31}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 408}, {"level": 2, "cluster": 1561}, {"level": 3, "cluster": 2050}]</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;ANTHROPOMORPHISM&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"SOCIAL SCIENCES"</data>
      <data key="d1">"Anthropomorphism is the attribution of human characteristics or behavior to non-human entities, discussed here as an abnormality in farming perception."&lt;SEP&gt;"The attribution of human traits or characteristics to nonhumans, used as a tool to interpret and discuss animal behavior."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-63d47730064116b6f4fe93ddb307901d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4d55b538adad3c639b729e3377b78a20</data>
