    <node id="&quot;VARROA MITE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"NORMAL_ENTITY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A destructive and adaptive parasite that attacks honey bees, with various genotypes including New Guinea and Korean variants."&lt;SEP&gt;"A destructive parasite that has significantly damaged the genetic diversity of the national bee herd and wiped out feral colonies."&lt;SEP&gt;"A parasite originating in Asia that destroys bee colonies and spread through Europe and eventually North America."&lt;SEP&gt;"A parasite that wreaked havoc across the beekeeping industry."&lt;SEP&gt;"A parasitic mite suggested as one of the contributors to the cause of colony collapse disorder."&lt;SEP&gt;"A parasitic mite that attacks honey bees, carrying the deformed wing virus and causing high colony mortality."&lt;SEP&gt;"A parasitic mite that infests honey bee colonies and can be treated with chemicals like Apistan and Miticur."&lt;SEP&gt;"A parasitic tick-shaped creature that infests honeybee colonies, feeding on hemolymph and spreading disabling viruses."&lt;SEP&gt;"A small, reddish-brown parasitic mite that infests honey bee colonies and poses a severe threat to beekeeping."&lt;SEP&gt;"Also known as Varroa jacobsonii, this is a parasite originating in Southeast Asia that affects honey bees, specifically Apis cerana and Apis mellifera."&lt;SEP&gt;"An external parasite of honey bees and brood that feeds on hemolymph."&lt;SEP&gt;"An external parasite of honey bees that causes colony damage, weight loss in bees, and can lead to colony mortality."&lt;SEP&gt;"An invasive parasitic mite that attaches to honey bees, causing significant population declines and forcing bees into a state of domestication."&lt;SEP&gt;"Varroa mite is a parasite affecting bees that has led to a decrease in honey production per hive."</data>
