    <node id="&quot;SUSTAINABILITY&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"SOCIAL SCIENCES"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A complex, contested ideal and social movement concerning the regulation of human use of nature and the reproduction of community, debated through frameworks like weak and strong sustainability."&lt;SEP&gt;"A concept and public conversation topic that acts as a 'banner' uniting diverse groups and causes, with ongoing debate about whether it encompasses only environmental impact or also social justice."&lt;SEP&gt;"A contested ideal and 'wicked problem' regarding the maintenance of socio-natural systems and resource availability over time."&lt;SEP&gt;"Discussed as both a norm related to functional integrity and as a social movement, involving decision-making processes and ethical trade-offs."&lt;SEP&gt;"Sustainability in this context is a moral and systemic goal for agriculture to minimize the consumption of natural resources to protect the ecology and future civilizations."&lt;SEP&gt;"Sustainability is a central concept discussed as a modern substitute for progress, linked to environmental consciousness and moral ideals."&lt;SEP&gt;"Sustainability is a concept concerned with the long-term environmental viability of food production systems."&lt;SEP&gt;"Sustainability is a conceptual framework for maintaining ecological balance and long-term viability, applied here to agricultural policy."&lt;SEP&gt;"Sustainability is a conceptual framework related to agrarian ideals and the significance of embodied experience in specific contexts."&lt;SEP&gt;"Sustainability is a contested concept used in environmental policies and corporate rhetoric, discussed here in terms of its substantive and nonsubstantive meanings."&lt;SEP&gt;"Sustainability is a contested ideal and concept concerning the impact of human activity on the environment and future generations, spanning agriculture, development, and architecture."&lt;SEP&gt;"Sustainability is the central concept discussed, analyzed through two lenses: the resource sufficiency approach (duration) and the functional integrity approach (system reproduction)."&lt;SEP&gt;"Sustainability is the central conceptual framework discussed, categorized into 'weak' and 'strong' approaches regarding the preservation of natural capital versus general welfare."&lt;SEP&gt;"Sustainability is the central conceptual framework discussed, involving empirical assessment, value judgments, and resource management."&lt;SEP&gt;"Sustainability refers to the capacity to maintain environmental balance and resource availability for future generations."&lt;SEP&gt;"The study and practice of maintaining ecological balance and avoiding the depletion of natural resources."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-553c0f788a5df6c23a481c0a3efdaabb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-416c88a4653bbd08275b4ad4b3f6d0d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c283b7bbdc3724574b4a465b7e4d29c6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a98ffdda5b456b304e6585d043b124d6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3a9d0dea50021742bc52cffc66eae1b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d63fd363bb5b8e772c6c0e59279b321&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d4b745ec575466c823b11b87211e1e2e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6efabe1598e6b04e35168d3c337aa67b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0cd2b14e898ba9fd4a8dd4ab14491966&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2b4618459cbb29170095bfe11ea386ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa6c175f37ce7089fa657f33595c666c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5004d71f44c231b6939419f18c324a3b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e15cd11d4bfedd81ebb62a5bb18b7913&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-11c2b2726c61ccaf67be15464365be01&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1c64b52d1680cf9b8c87710eaab068d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3ad5716c9ff65294108cb908fec2e2c8</data>
      <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 8}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 156}, {"level": 2, "cluster": 817}, {"level": 3, "cluster": 1850}]</data>
    </node>
