    <node id="&quot;JAMES ANDERSON&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A great Scottish agriculturalist and theorist of ground rent, born in Edinburgh, who invented the Scottish plough and described the rotation of grassland."&lt;SEP&gt;"An author who wrote essays relating to agriculture and rural affairs in the late 18th century."&lt;SEP&gt;"An author whose work from the 1777 edition regarding plant palatability is cited."&lt;SEP&gt;"An individual who, in 1777, taught that paddocks should be grazed again 14-19 days after the preceding grazing."&lt;SEP&gt;"James Anderson is a Scottish author who reported and documented the Swedish experiments on palatability indices in his 1777 edition."&lt;SEP&gt;"James Anderson is an author and agriculturalist who wrote about rational grazing and the rotation of pastures in the 18th century."&lt;SEP&gt;"James Anderson was a Scottish farmer of the eighteenth century who advocated for the division of cattle into groups to optimize pasture use and economy."</data>
