    <node id="&quot;RESOURCE SUFFICIENCY&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"SOCIAL SCIENCES"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A conceptual approach to sustainability often associated with accounting-based methods and the discipline of economics."&lt;SEP&gt;"A general view of sustainability where a practice is considered sustainable if the necessary resources are foreseeably available."&lt;SEP&gt;"A leading idea for conceptualizing sustainability focused on maintaining enough resources to support human welfare."&lt;SEP&gt;"An approach to sustainability that focuses on ensuring a minimal amount of resources is available to individuals to experience freedom and well-being."&lt;SEP&gt;"An economic and ecological concept focused on calculating and achieving enough resources to sustain a population."&lt;SEP&gt;"Resource Sufficiency is a conceptual framework for assessing sustainability by measuring the rate of resource consumption against available stocks over a specific time frame."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6efabe1598e6b04e35168d3c337aa67b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ddd79b341dfcb637bbc86f0265484513&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-11c852a940991461ca29b76becfe24a7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5004d71f44c231b6939419f18c324a3b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b96ca94eb3fc51223443470c6a960c19&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89231dc9df6367dfeaa7e74d7dd3e375</data>
