    </node>
    <node id="&quot;FUNCTIONAL INTEGRITY&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"SOCIAL SCIENCES"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A concept describing the mechanisms that allow whole systems, such as societies or ecosystems, to regenerate and maintain stability over time."&lt;SEP&gt;"A framework for sustainability based on system modeling and ecologists' perspectives, emphasizing a community ideal and a promising basis for public discourse."&lt;SEP&gt;"A leading idea for conceptualizing sustainability focused on the integrity of biological systems and resilient patterns of interaction."&lt;SEP&gt;"A mode of thinking used by Jefferson that prioritizes the economic and political cohesion of a society to prevent internal conflict."&lt;SEP&gt;"Functional Integrity is a conceptual approach to sustainability used to assess whether a system can maintain its essential functions over time."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-6efabe1598e6b04e35168d3c337aa67b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ddd79b341dfcb637bbc86f0265484513&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5004d71f44c231b6939419f18c324a3b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b96ca94eb3fc51223443470c6a960c19&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89231dc9df6367dfeaa7e74d7dd3e375</data>
