    <node id="&quot;EUROPEAN HONEY BEE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"NORMAL_ENTITY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A honey bee species (Apis mellifera) with larger worker-comb cell diameters and longer developmental times compared to its Eastern and Africanized cousins."&lt;SEP&gt;"A species of bee characterized by specific behaviors and a high capacity for cooperation within a colony."&lt;SEP&gt;"A species of bee not native to North America, imported as early as 1622."&lt;SEP&gt;"A species of bee that adapted well to the New World."&lt;SEP&gt;"A species of bee that is highly susceptible to varroa mites and is now largely dependent on human beekeepers for survival."&lt;SEP&gt;"A species of honey bee that serves as the host for varroa mites and is managed by beekeepers."&lt;SEP&gt;"A subspecies of honey bee (Apis mellifera) that has developed mechanisms for surviving cold winter months in northern climes, including the formation of winter clusters."&lt;SEP&gt;"The species of bee that hosts the varroa mite and is the subject of genetic and management efforts to ensure survival."&lt;SEP&gt;"The species of honey bee used in the research regarding varroa mite attraction to drone brood."</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-e823f4271cc674d258a850b82bd8b70b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ca8cfd267b6fa9e2212b61e34235db38&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8201a44e8406b76d66752ef2bda352f5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4c100bb043929fe5d53e4af2e279dfb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fbe3628e117427ed1c35a791b026e9a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9b9c3131c2e6763d0e21d39ae068acb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9eafb5519ae847ebc95f1b6bd7a3901&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-027615784640b56f822e7f3e6ed8eb5c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-28ac9aabed87efda91436e0c9b5ff311</data>
