    <node id="&quot;KING STROPHARIA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"NORMAL_ENTITY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A mushroom species (Stropharia rugosoannulata) that fruits primarily in the summer and requires frequent watering."&lt;SEP&gt;"A mushroom species (Stropharia rugosoannulata) that is exquisitely edible when young but loses flavor with maturity."&lt;SEP&gt;"A mushroom species (Stropharia rugosoannulata) that produces rhizomorphic mycelia."&lt;SEP&gt;"A mushroom species known for its adaptability to substrates and its ability to form tenacious mycelial mats."&lt;SEP&gt;"A mushroom species that can be grown on the same substrate sequence as Shiitake and Oyster mushrooms."&lt;SEP&gt;"A mushroom species that can express mutations when transferred on media for more than ten petri dishes."&lt;SEP&gt;"A mushroom species used as a comparison for outdoor bed growth conditions."&lt;SEP&gt;"A predictable species of mushroom used as a point of comparison for the behavior and predictability of other fungi."&lt;SEP&gt;"A species of gourmet mushroom that typically lasts 3 to 4 years on a hardwood chip base."&lt;SEP&gt;"A species of mushroom (Stropharia rugosoannulata) known for its ability to grow in various substrate formulations."&lt;SEP&gt;"A species of mushroom used in cultivation and production."&lt;SEP&gt;"A type of mushroom (S. rugosoannulata) characterized by its flavor quality, growth via rhizomorphs, and use in gourmet cooking."&lt;SEP&gt;"A type of mushroom whose mycelium typically has a grayish white appearance before flushing with bright white."&lt;SEP&gt;"Also known as Stropharia rugosoannulata, a mushroom with a musty, phenolic smell on grain and a woodsy odor on sawdust."&lt;SEP&gt;"Also known as Stropharia rugosoannulata, an edible mushroom used as a base medium for fish food and as a biological filter for water runoff."&lt;SEP&gt;"Common name for Stropharia rugosoannulata."&lt;SEP&gt;"King Stropharia (Stropharia rugosoannulata) are primary decomposers typically found in woodlands."&lt;SEP&gt;"Mushrooms of the species Stropharia rugosoannulata that require a living community of microflora to produce a fruitbody."</data>
