      <data key="d0">"NORMAL_ENTITY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A species of fungus, also known as Reishi, which is the subject of the cultivation process described in the text."&lt;SEP&gt;"Also known as Reishi, a fungus known for its medicinal properties and various cultivation methods."&lt;SEP&gt;"Also known as Reishi, a polypore mushroom recommended for stump cultivation for its antiviral and anticancer properties."&lt;SEP&gt;"Also known as Reishi, this is a species of fungus characterized by its cap and stalk development, which is sensitive to carbon dioxide levels and light."&lt;SEP&gt;"Also known as Reishi, this is a woody mushroom species that produces a rust-colored spore cloud and shows a phototropic response to light."&lt;SEP&gt;"Also known as Reishi, this mushroom species has a white mycelium that matures into yellow as it colonizes agar medium."&lt;SEP&gt;"Ganoderma lucidum is a fungal species that produces abundant secondary metabolites."&lt;SEP&gt;"Ganoderma lucidum is a medicinal mushroom mentioned as being beneficial when taken with vitamin C."&lt;SEP&gt;"Ganoderma lucidum is a mushroom from which unique antiviral components have been isolated."&lt;SEP&gt;"Ganoderma lucidum is the scientific name for the Reishi mushroom, known for its mesmerizing transformations and medicinal value."&lt;SEP&gt;"Known as Reishi or Ling Chi, a medicinal mushroom species."&lt;SEP&gt;"The scientific name for Reishi mushrooms, known for producing dense mycelia and varying appearances."&lt;SEP&gt;"The scientific name for the Reishi mushroom, used as a primary saprophyte in cultivation cycles."&lt;SEP&gt;"The type mushroom of its genus, found in warmer regions and growing on oaks and hardwoods."</data>
