    <node id="&quot;AGARICUS BRUNNESCENS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"NORMAL_ENTITY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A mushroom species synonymous with Agaricus bisporus, known for its Portobello form and used extensively in the mushroom industry."&lt;SEP&gt;"A mushroom species, also known as Agaricus bisporus, which includes the Button mushroom and Portobello varieties, widely distributed in temperate regions."&lt;SEP&gt;"A species of mushroom, also known as the Button or Portobello mushroom, which is widely cultivated but contains agaritine, a potential carcinogen."&lt;SEP&gt;"A species of mushroom, also known as the Portobello or Button mushroom, characterized by its spores and gill tissue that are not unsavory."&lt;SEP&gt;"A specific species of Button mushroom grown in wooden tray cultures on commercial farms."&lt;SEP&gt;"A specific species of mushroom for which commercial spawn is produced."&lt;SEP&gt;"Agaricus brunnescens is a mushroom species used as a reference for composting methods and biological comparisons."&lt;SEP&gt;"Also known as the Button mushroom, a species grown on cased, sterilized grain from bottles."&lt;SEP&gt;"Also known as the White Button mushroom, it is a species that requires microflora and is excluded from certain sterile spore-mass inoculation techniques."&lt;SEP&gt;"Known as the Portobello or Button Mushroom, a species used in cultivation."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Button Mushroom, also known as Blanco-Bellos or Portobello Mushroom; it is the most widely cultivated mushroom in the world."&lt;SEP&gt;"The scientific name for the Button mushroom."&lt;SEP&gt;"The scientific name for the mushroom species, including the Portobello form, discussed in the text."&lt;SEP&gt;"The scientific name for the white Button mushroom, which has been genetically selected for thick flesh, small gills, and a short stem to extend shelf life."</data>
