      <data key="d0">"NORMAL_ENTITY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A mushroom species (Flammulina velutipes) used as a baseline for cultivation strategies and harvest methods for H. capnoides."&lt;SEP&gt;"A mushroom species whose mycelium is noted for smelling like week-old dirty socks."&lt;SEP&gt;"A popular cultivated mushroom in Japan, closely ranked with Shiitake and Pholiota nameko."&lt;SEP&gt;"A popular cultivated mushroom in Japan, ranked closely with Shiitake and Nameko."&lt;SEP&gt;"A species of mushroom cultivated using specific inoculation paths involving sawdust and grain spawn."&lt;SEP&gt;"A species of mushroom that consumes supplemented sawdust, triggering exothermic reactions during incubation."&lt;SEP&gt;"A type of mushroom cultivated using various hardwoods, including oak."&lt;SEP&gt;"A type of mushroom whose mycelium typically has a grayish white appearance before flushing with bright white."&lt;SEP&gt;"A wood-loving mushroom species (Flammulina velutipes) cultivated using a specific sawdust/bran formula."&lt;SEP&gt;"Also known as Nametake, Yuki-motase, the Winter Mushroom, the Velvet or Furry Foot Collybia, or the Golden Mushroom; it is a mushroom species (Flammulina velutipes) characterized by its sensitivity to light and CO2, typically growing on hardwoods."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enokitake (Flammulina velutipes) is a type of mushroom that produces the polysaccharide flammulin."&lt;SEP&gt;"Enokitake is a species of mushroom that can produce fast fruitings on alder and cottonwood."&lt;SEP&gt;"The Japanese name for the Enoki mushroom, meaning 'The Snow Peak Mushroom'."</data>
