    <node id="&quot;RATTAN LAL&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A researcher and scientist known for his work on soil health and no-till farming methods."&lt;SEP&gt;"A scientist and professor at Ohio State University specializing in conservation agriculture, soil carbon sequestration, and food security."&lt;SEP&gt;"A soil scientist and expert in conservation agriculture who studied the effects of tillage on soil fertility and erosion."&lt;SEP&gt;"An expert or researcher who, like Kofi Boa, criticizes the practice of bare-ground no-till."&lt;SEP&gt;"Rattan Lal is a knowledgeable individual and acquaintance of the author met at the 'Dig It' symposium."&lt;SEP&gt;"Rattan Lal is a researcher or expert whose ideas on soil health parallel those of Beck."&lt;SEP&gt;"Rattan Lal is a researcher who estimated the societal value of carbon at $120 per ton."&lt;SEP&gt;"Rattan Lal is a scientist who has estimated the amount of carbon lost by the world's agricultural soils and the sequestration potential of conservation agriculture."&lt;SEP&gt;"Rattan Lal is a scientist who published a paper in 2006 analyzing the relationship between soil organic carbon and crop yields for wheat, rice, and maize."&lt;SEP&gt;"Rattan Lal is a soft-spoken soil scientist from Ohio State University who advocates for putting carbon back into the ground to improve soil fertility and offset CO2 emissions."&lt;SEP&gt;"Rattan Lal is an expert cited regarding the varying effectiveness and quality of no-till systems."&lt;SEP&gt;"Rattan Lal is the author and narrator of the text, an expert who conducts fieldwork in the rural tropics and reflects on agricultural challenges in Africa."</data>
