    <node id="&quot;NO-TILL&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"TECHNOLOGY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A farming method that avoids soil disturbance, focusing on maintaining soil health and moisture through mulch and cover crops."&lt;SEP&gt;"A farming method that avoids soil tillage to preserve soil structure, increase water absorption, and reduce erosion."&lt;SEP&gt;"A farming practice at the heart of conservation agriculture that avoids plowing to leave crop residues as soil cover."&lt;SEP&gt;"A farming technique that avoids primary tillage to protect soil structure and fertility."&lt;SEP&gt;"No-till is a farming method that avoids disturbing the soil through tillage to preserve organic matter and soil structure."&lt;SEP&gt;"No-till is a farming method that avoids mechanical tillage to prevent soil degradation and protect mycorrhizal fungi."&lt;SEP&gt;"No-till is a farming method that avoids primary tillage to reduce soil erosion and improve soil structure."&lt;SEP&gt;"No-till is a farming system and practice that avoids tillage to reduce soil erosion and improve long-term land health."&lt;SEP&gt;"No-till is a farming technique that avoids disturbing the soil through tillage to save water and preserve soil structure."&lt;SEP&gt;"No-till is a farming technology and practice that involves planting crops without plowing the soil to reduce erosion and degradation."&lt;SEP&gt;"No-till is a farming technology/method where the soil is left undisturbed, avoiding traditional plowing to maintain soil structure and organic matter."&lt;SEP&gt;"No-till is an agricultural practice of planting crops without plowing the soil to preserve soil structure and organic matter."&lt;SEP&gt;"No-till is an agricultural technique that avoids disturbing the soil through tillage, saving time and improving soil health."&lt;SEP&gt;"No-till is an agricultural technique that avoids primary tillage to maintain soil structure, moisture, and organic matter."</data>
