    <node id="&quot;TERRA PRETA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"TECHNOLOGY"</data>
      <data key="d1">"A type of fertile, dark soil found around Amazonian villages, serving as a model for combining organic matter and biochar."&lt;SEP&gt;"An ancient method of creating highly fertile, dark anthropogenic soil through the addition of charcoal and organic matter."&lt;SEP&gt;"An ancient type of dark, fertile, man-made soil from the Amazon Basin used as a model for modern soil-building recipes."&lt;SEP&gt;"Ancient, nutrient-rich black soils created by native Amazonians through the addition of organic matter, charcoal, and ash."&lt;SEP&gt;"Terra preta is a type of anthropogenic, fertile black soil created by Amazonian Indians."&lt;SEP&gt;"Terra preta refers to a type of highly fertile, dark anthropogenic soil, which Kring aims to emulate on a smaller scale using biochar."&lt;SEP&gt;"Terra preta refers to anthropogenic dark earths created by humans, demonstrating that soil organic matter can be increased."&lt;SEP&gt;"Terra preta refers to highly fertile, anthropogenic dark earths found in the Amazon, used here as a benchmark for high soil carbon levels."</data>
