    </node>
    <node id="&quot;NORTH DAKOTA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">North Dakota is a U.S. state located in the center of North America, characterized by its semi-arid northern plains, prairies, and a continental climate. The state is defined by a history of agricultural booms and busts, featuring rural farming communities, large farms, and a western region experiencing an oil boom, though it also faces challenges such as population drain and a struggling agricultural economy. Notable landmarks and locations within the state include the town of Gackle and Menoken Farm.

As a major honey-producing region, North Dakota serves as a critical hub for migratory beekeeping. John Miller maintains holding yards and honey-production bee yards there, spending his summers producing honey from clover, alfalfa, buckwheat, and goldenrod. While the state is used by migratory beekeepers for hive placement and is a source of bees transported to California for almond pollination, the commercial beekeeping industry has declined significantly. This decline is compounded by issues such as the spread of mites, severe colony losses resulting from cold summers, and a period where North Dakota bees were banned from entering California due to tracheal mite infestations. In this environment, large-scale beekeepers have secured most of the prime territory, and the North Dakota Department of Agriculture oversees regional operations.

The state is also a center for agricultural research and sustainable farming. It is the location where Kristine Nichols conducted research and gained extensive experience with the USDA and the Natural Resources Conservation Service. Furthermore, North Dakota is where Gabe Brown operates his sustainable ranch and sells products at farmers' markets; his family originally settled in the state in the 1880s. Other residents include Harry and Brenda Krause, as well as Wessel and various European settlers. The region's agricultural viability is noted for its short-season corn and soybean varieties, and its farmers are noted to share commonalities with those in Costa Rica.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3ab2196ed3cfc357ea2968eacf70e5cf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a2f9bf7db953b160f0ff4e9bdf3bd0c9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-47bc4d6bb9b54f7a4498039a09e27192&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dff8f6d16d91f6c080ca2e3b7cb50555&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7acff18895bfa08e6388fc660689bad7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc6b42805d670ff5f1b75194ea815c5e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1791122186a3d77110cdc1caf21599f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dda09812f219e231d083bae5447467aa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-faafe8310294120e238fe933148dfbad&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-21be92c21b7215c9e86b338cd15d5f1f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d268b118d865a964f0911adbcd24b46&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-875244dbf5b92a0cbabb5deff3c5629e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-708bae7fc6ca7f9031d5fe8940db78f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-13a019e529b263129037d3a41686a1bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a9e3bb2a40d743661733d6555e921ace&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64b1d9cb4feb594eafc5f44559345e22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5780ec82bf5f9eace3f594f7e7368b9c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1bfda3cdec46f8ab5a386ecf15ae6edb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-12ca6ad40e2f56cfc0ab67f96d1552b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c53dab54c5e432f26775d35bbbc12efa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-52fd5c92980e8846f221b5900200a76f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50d84e8388b1b3835cf15d2729a7bded&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-edf21622f766ab205d57f7915f9105bc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2c4b89fc508843db93c9d0cde5f230d7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85eaeec0849996dd41a39cefe3eb69e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ceb6afb7b86fd8dee5d1ffc49066a3be&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-69a6f293580aa424d04ad67558a1427b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d8286e6a8f25ded00034a7a287f09cf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0984220078ce71973ba3b0fa10b37a2e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6deba29b237640ada67472e874a1f037&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0455c588ed816ff0b5f445247e46aade&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5dd6250bf9119073ff90517ec6a09534&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-253af24cc5cc2d7d952a8f5e4a183029&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e6a3d7885d5c7d0196e5f8b1d626686d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3e97945a272b68a7aa63d93a54b8c6ce&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8f20e140e1c799a2e1a35a7c9720b9c5</data>
      <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 6}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 118}, {"level": 2, "cluster": 716}, {"level": 3, "cluster": 1829}, {"level": 4, "cluster": 2100}]</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;TERESA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">Teresa is the wife, college sweetheart, and partner of the author (Joel Salatin), as well as the mother of Daniel and Rachel. Holding a degree in home economics, Teresa is a skilled culinary expert who has cooked from scratch and made her own granola from raw ingredients for thirty years to maintain health and save money. She is credited with upholding traditional homemaking and spiritual values, which includes using reusable containers to avoid plastic wrap and managing the food preservation, canning, and freezing of produce at Polyface Farm.

Professionally, Teresa is a co-owner of T&amp;E Meats and a partner in Salatin&amp;Cloud LLC; she and the author co-purchased half ownership of a federally inspected abattoir. Having been involved in the early stages of starting their farm in 1982, she runs the family farm alongside the author and has received a conservation award. Together, the couple previously spent ten years as vegetarians and contemplated homeschooling their children. Throughout their partnership, Teresa is described as a supportive presence who manages the household's food supplies—from maintaining a stash of home-canned goods to keeping hamburger patties on hand—and is occasionally noted for her alarm regarding the author's extensive book purchases.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-9970f491e374fd37ac081ba7e9f57b3b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b98d36e0446cb7ec45975df5969effe3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-58c9172daf9801bd62c5822cc8c36ab4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c977d18fdd696e1f826cc649c3444b7d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e1c7e1b601a6b8a0deb46da88c1a33b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-244b46a035b1d5494275c24656572f4d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3fe8a4b8462310a0c1d2d68cbd07f35b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a8cf7a7ce24ea8608d39a989016a49ce&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-873f333c230e885057265c220512e61c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa91d64a86fa58173080c8817943c1c0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-97df03a76764ebe5533b42a54e7596c5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c02f2d11c9242c1e4114777275fe1d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a222f4d2ebac0805d7a35f6ea2281563&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22f3967d311f087b86fc91bd0708142c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-05374d444d89278cd804bde3807b7797&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-650e78b8096f9f7cece370950962f3c8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-de4b66d69047666bdb2ab47a03828fc8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-880c95881d83db855d75eadf8297358a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f116a0e83303d4ebb8971bd977ade7c2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-01c91bc35d36c8498b9d61a9d491bf15&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7342c811b61a9c3bb8956112826bc2d6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c04b9bcf35a50dc6d1720961891fccef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b87537885c268eea02e5c3eda3297f5b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ac85fc649d6253fd56f0c0e65b99ddfe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1a9921cf42c52cb58653d22cae4ded08&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-947ecff2bb83daf7b7263f98f4a72ef8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-42d3a39282037fb0810e4ffb4ed03f81</data>
      <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 7}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 139}, {"level": 2, "cluster": 757}, {"level": 3, "cluster": 1839}]</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;FLORIDA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">Florida is a warm, southern U.S. state located within hardiness Zone 9, making it a primary location for migratory beekeepers to winter their hives and raise queens due to early pollen flows. Because of its climate, Florida is known for producing package bees and serves as a source of uniform matrons for requeening. Commercial hives and honey bees are frequently transported from Florida to California for almond pollination, and some beekeepers utilize a bee sharecropper system to rent colonies for the summer.

The state has a complex history with apiculture; it is believed that many bee scourges, including varroa mites, originated there, and it reported bee hive "dead-outs" as early as 1915. More recently, Florida was the location where Dave Hackenberg operated his bees and maintained winter apiaries, experiencing significant colony collapse and bee disappearances, including an instance where 2,900 shipped hives were affected. In response to these challenges, some beekeepers in the state reported success in keeping varroa-infested colonies alive using screened bottom boards and powdered sugar. Additionally, the small hive beetle is considered a significant pest in the state, and beekeepers must contend with venomous snakes that often hide under hives.

Geographically and biologically, Florida represents the eastern edge of the band where Africanized honey bees are found. It is also a site for botanical and mycological study, being the location where *Agaricus blazei* was first collected and grown outdoors in mound culture, and where a specific strain of Oyster mushroom (*P. florida*) was identified.

Legally and socially, Florida passed a law in 2009 prohibiting the use of additives such as corn syrup in products labeled as honey. The state is also mentioned in the context of local law enforcement, specifically regarding a raid on an individual selling raw dairy. Finally, Florida's agricultural cycles can be impacted by weather anomalies, such as periods where temperatures hover below freezing for two weeks, which negatively affects honey flows.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-2f3d99de4967b60e7e74b3639abc430c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dff8f6d16d91f6c080ca2e3b7cb50555&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b435ef7667f374b0d5824cdcad97ba20&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9dac5e0fc4b3efb7d62d286bfe82fe84&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a9465e78f869f57a714827f88ed9c132&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ca8cfd267b6fa9e2212b61e34235db38&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-88b98e2001c5ae7faad90b16ac45f0cf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-75673023cb192cee1fbe66473e0ff86d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e1b198fa71b44d8dd33dcfc2eb267be7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5780ec82bf5f9eace3f594f7e7368b9c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b6278bcba2e2dfe6f1202b147f87019&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-52fd5c92980e8846f221b5900200a76f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50d84e8388b1b3835cf15d2729a7bded&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ab6cfebf002fae160c578679ffc1aa29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6246333458a48f952d940bddf47976a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa20c48947aea0538518c8462c9c2696&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-936929886afd4a64ba379fe6ac052a32&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7ac66f2a993a8f84ec2280130d78e1c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-82d288912e79b43bbf8f55e37e96c89c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ceb6afb7b86fd8dee5d1ffc49066a3be&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-880657fcead983c1ada31e3024872a79&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d6710a74adf9d60f7c9121567c01098&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d62560fba9415ed49fcc7977635cc69b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-01c91bc35d36c8498b9d61a9d491bf15&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5dd6250bf9119073ff90517ec6a09534&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8422f27b6e2f7c49af2d7c0895af1179&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6f025d035915467517ca424f6e1fea96</data>
      <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 12}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 216}, {"level": 2, "cluster": 1016}]</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;VERMONT&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">Vermont is a U.S. state located in the northern/northeastern United States, characterized by long, cold winters and a population that is among the lowest in the country. Geographically, it contains the Green Mountains, the Champlain Valley, and Camel's Hump, and it includes locations such as South Burlington and South Village. The state is known for its roadside pumpkin displays that attract tourists and buyers, and it is noted as a place where buying fresh sweet corn in January is unusual.

In the realm of agriculture and beekeeping, Vermont serves as a significant setting for the author's observations and practices. The author moved to Vermont in 1988 (returning after time spent with the Bear Tribe) to keep bees and manage hives, positioning apiaries in sunny locations to combat the harsh climate. In this northern climate region, brood area placement in Top Bar Hives (TBH) is critical for winter survival, and colonies typically require 60 to 100 pounds of honey to survive the winter. Specific honey harvesting, mite treatment schedules, and the creation of nucleus colonies in May or early June are practiced for optimal winter preparation. The state is also where the author observed that wax moth damage typically begins in late June and early July, and where *Varroa destructor* first appeared in the early 1990s. Additionally, the author noted that the bear population in Vermont has doubled, which has impacted beekeeping practices.

Vermont is home to several institutions and businesses, including Middlebury College, the Intervale Center Farm Incubator, and the Northern Grain Growers Association. It is the base for various beekeeping entities such as Champlain Valley Apiaries, Lapham's Bay Apiaries, and Dancing Bee Gardens, the latter of which provides pollination services. Furthermore, Vermont is where Bill Murphy introduced Voisin's grazing management ideas and where the author worked for a large commercial beekeeper and appeared on a radio talk show to discuss organic food prices.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3fda7a4926edf81f63ac6018a79d56da&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4f0965a7f888753898bdb3624830c9fd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a5cfe3323511c9b97faca58e44619a3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a80590371c3ba416132df035a1f48358&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b96aee8fce1383e3a88948dc5d0aad4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-58c9172daf9801bd62c5822cc8c36ab4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-141bd4f59be1ccee796abafaf0658f22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cee44e954d1abb009f061fd993b844c8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ca8cfd267b6fa9e2212b61e34235db38&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-36349dc9e2604b53b31c0c3d9931128e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-875244dbf5b92a0cbabb5deff3c5629e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1de5be48f8b86dabc08e5087f75750aa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-931d0f7479e9dd63e66694c8dd0f68bd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cfd05a12ef78763e74306325feeb5eac&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fb33ca6e0dec58803e47a21bf100c03&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-22f0ad20db2374dde44294c481f26b21&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6696f8ef99d58e5e15f540cf5edf86a9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea68cfbbe3fc012d7e18af95936eb15e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f30f69a81886f658de51b870faedd598&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4f970a1d154acdb2ae156eac6a317018&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d4150698732f393c235b8b3e1cb2d88&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6287c81b3be930766b61c0ad32bf562f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b464827b98fd832772a9e2806f166940&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-abf15980aea2d79d85e102a8e8c947bc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3bd6606d64af56a1c2f7c398c12f78f1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-622c003f3eb63c8fc5cb7dc192c0e76d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-955ad3356ae65234e36469cc7de4b3e5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-086256446757da0fffa536b4247d998d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0c7079e4e94fbb77a10c466d9f4a5438&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e962d9ff8064282a92c3166d68acbc0f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-80cb5b40207cce260add50ac65980ec6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0f1544f96bf3906c3ffd91d3f551a278&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b1e6b92e2d699a91a2124de7f67662fc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dbfbbce7bbb2b1195a5322a8057953dc</data>
