    <node id="&quot;USDA&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"ORGANIZATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">The United States Department of Agriculture (USDA) is a federal executive department and government agency responsible for developing and executing policies, laws, and regulations related to farming, forestry, food, and natural resources. The USDA oversees agricultural production and food safety, including the inspection of processing plants and kitchens, the approval of food labels, and the establishment of federal inspection standards and nutritional profiles. It is responsible for grading and quality certification, providing size and packaging standards for various agricultural produce, and implementing food safety guidelines (GAPs) and mandatory rules, such as those for almond pasteurization.

A central role of the USDA is the management of organic standards and accreditation. The agency codifies national organic standards, manages the National Organic Program (NOP), oversees the use of the organic logo, and approves third-party certification and inspection agencies. Additionally, the USDA establishes certification standards for grass-fed meat.

As a research and data hub, the USDA houses research laboratories and employs numerous scientists, including Don Reicosky, Sara Wright, and Randy Anderson. The agency conducts a wide array of studies, ranging from 1930s research on sod-farming pastures and the 1929 circular on mushroom growing to contemporary research on weed pressure and queen bee insemination. It provides essential horticultural data, including the publication of the Yearbook of Agriculture and the maintenance of plant hardiness zone maps used to categorize climate regions. The USDA also provides economic estimates for honeybee production, crop statistics, and data on the growth of farmers' markets.

The USDA provides significant financial and administrative support, offering grants for community gardening projects—such as those for Janus Youth, St. Johns Woods, and Village Gardens—and providing reimbursements to schools for meals served. It also facilitates the "Farm to School" initiative to link schools with local food producers, though its procurement regulations are sometimes cited as a challenge for these institutions.

The agency also manages international agricultural trade and safety, including regulating the import of bee hives and approving transgenic crops and animal feed additives like methyl palmitate.

Despite its broad mandate, the USDA has faced criticism. It has been described as a laggard in promoting soil health practices, criticized for encouraging the feeding of carrion to herbivores, and accused of promoting a "cheap food policy" that neglects its constituency. Some authors note that the agency has historically championed agrochemical farming and emphasized corporate partnerships and product development over systems research.</data>
