    <node id="&quot;JEFFERSON&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">Thomas Jefferson was a foundational American figure, the third President of the United States, and the author of the Declaration of Independence, "Notes on the State of Virginia," and the "Farm Book." As a political leader and philosopher, Jefferson served as the patron of agrarian political philosophy, envisioning a republic centered on the interests of farmers to ensure the stability, sustainability, and general welfare of the nation.

Jefferson advocated for an agrarian society, operating his own farm at Monticello, and viewed farming as a marriage of personal and national interests. He believed that agriculture was crucial to the development of virtuous character in citizens, famously promoting the philosophical view that farmers make the best citizens. By linking farming with citizenship and democracy, Jefferson emphasized key virtues such as self-reliance, community solidarity, and the independence and liberty of individuals within a free society. He viewed this agrarian model as a means to create a unified and stable economy and as a social goal for the emerging nation.

Beyond his agricultural ideals, Jefferson was an advocate for human rights who discussed the necessity of "chains" to prevent the government from becoming criminal and the need for periodic revolutions to maintain a healthy republic. His intellectual bequest continues to influence contemporary views on environmental ethics, conservation agriculture, and community-oriented ethics. As an intellectual forebear of the agrarian tradition and figures like Wendell Berry, Thomas Jefferson remains a symbol of rural American ideals whose conceptualizations of land-use and the "farmer-citizen" serve as a model for social and political hope.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-3b08c5812a78d79a773ecd56c3c1e084&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7dbc1c2ec064a792c47e97c74d0a8614&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bbb61501e645127f9021b6ed20f37476&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6d5818cdf2378e6f878f29ebbca00962&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a8d3828434db854107ffe391485c26e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd1c1d4f15102d0ce3bf4f9a157c71f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd6cef65b73cbf090d0f46d890908a11&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8adcb8e80a538f06909fb931e2ebec15&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-84729b070482e322c7c832ced520e10b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3f55b24ad3e4ece28934dea2092a4f72&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-21251d93da08e46a2457c1d8ac3cc530&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-72ac4184da0b36e65982eb7c72e1e025&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff197400ac8e94cfb08419bd8a6c660a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d86b4a55af4fdaec0c4c33982e78baf4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4225696c39528f2ce0e21784032e94f8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4b97dd6d6b95eae2e9e3d26bad764c5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7ebf23631547617747b7c2b24e2249cf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3a496eb9c6c8d4c66c22b3ac65fe5b31&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-00790ac56a654c0cfb0da5632ad72e10&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89231dc9df6367dfeaa7e74d7dd3e375&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44b869f980d6664b6e8d9930d9e12a16&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a488dc13c8287677db8f5b116ebb1a1b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eb807e6a60ab003ee9b4b47c9c83c846&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff7ff846be03f8677673730625ad6e54&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4953d5aa366add6d40468e1643931eaa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1e7bc5a59a0a74b310624922fb53f5b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1c613cbad49e71f1cd416e26e34150f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5add464238772f14d15e0e73bc4ad2f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf669185fd520e9a112a6a92f50cf468&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f4cb059595fad783966d73f011af493a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1a9921cf42c52cb58653d22cae4ded08&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e15cd11d4bfedd81ebb62a5bb18b7913&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3330698527e14dc575dc6a2e0e2a7c9d</data>
      <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 3}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 86}, {"level": 2, "cluster": 638}, {"level": 3, "cluster": 1817}]</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;VARROA DESTRUCTOR&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"NORMAL_ENTITY"</data>
      <data key="d1">*Varroa destructor*, commonly known as the varroa mite, is a highly pathogenic and invasive species of parasitic arachnid originating from the Korean peninsula. Identified as the primary biological threat to honey bee populations globally, it is often described as the "bane of the beekeeping industry" and represents the single greatest challenge to honey bee colonies and the honey industry.

This mite infests honey bee hives by attacking both adult bees and their larvae. Once attached, *Varroa destructor* feeds on the bees, which suppresses their immune systems and leads to physical deformities and weight loss. Beyond direct predation, the mite acts as a vector for spreading viruses and other diseases, which can ultimately result in total colony collapse if not managed.

To combat this pest, beekeepers and researchers employ various control methods, including the use of formic acid, oxalic acid, heat treatment techniques, and specialized traps. Additionally, breeders are working to create honey bee populations with innate resistance to the mite by selecting for hygienic behavior. Though sometimes compared to tracheal mites, *Varroa destructor* is a larger species and is recognized as the more significant driver of colony crashes.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-68758adec1ce1aa5af3106fe56e7d365&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-54c5f82f0f44cdd87998f2211f5330e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1b96aee8fce1383e3a88948dc5d0aad4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c53fadbf74ed2ba310e067e40c2b8ca&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bb725f31999b5452e1ba0388600c6589&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-817bac06738b1392b1904426ad3a9798&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ca8cfd267b6fa9e2212b61e34235db38&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89a40cc1637c10e1845e7fbc6db9ac2e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5cdc449839e793310ef8f89b87479c09&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e31a42be32b10fec891321276eff3bb9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-931d0f7479e9dd63e66694c8dd0f68bd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e09b1967f42adef7d483602f8085943&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c53dab54c5e432f26775d35bbbc12efa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-35f6eef0d2ff08b3a3b7b2c2e942edde&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c5e37349454eb3591b4615887c1807b8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2f592015c17fe4fa51a988e96c438765&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4f970a1d154acdb2ae156eac6a317018&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2691225b766f240d4e630fa426b6e435&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-09c586403b9fbc7d85f3084e5a000eb0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27c4227735c6f952b192f6d769deb712&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7e8f2ee1522e3b4a6c4f7618a9db21e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4642364014d20a8527d9fb4ec06bdb04&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4fe964bb050fa238a66a95e9fc7e0164</data>
