    <node id="&quot;OYSTER MUSHROOMS&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"NORMAL_ENTITY"</data>
      <data key="d1">Oyster mushrooms, encompassing species from the genera *Pleurotus* (such as *P. ostreatus*, *P. pulmonarius*, and *P. citrinopileatus*) and *Hypsizygus*, are highly adaptive, edible gourmet fungi prized for their nutritional value. Naturally occurring as primary decomposers of woodland deadwood and newly felled trees, these mushrooms are known for their aromatic mycelium and unique nematode-trapping abilities.

In cultivation, oyster mushrooms are versatile and can be grown on a wide variety of substrates, including logs, stumps, cereal straws, paper products, and grain spawn. They are typically grown using bag, column, wall, bottle, or perforated plastic container cultures rather than trays, as they evolve on vertical surfaces. While they thrive in environments with a neutral pH—distinguishing them from acidophilic contaminant fungi—they exhibit strong photosensitivity; without light exposure, they may malform into coral-like structures. Their growth rates peak at specific carbon dioxide levels, and they can produce mushrooms directly after colonization, sometimes forming primordia at specific sites such as holes in plastic columns.

From a management perspective, oyster mushrooms are characterized by tenacious mycelium and are often used as a cultivation comparison point against other species like Shiitake and Maitake. They are recommended for reinoculating substrate after Shiitake cultivation. However, they present specific challenges: they produce a very high spore load during the cropping cycle, which can interfere with fan operation and act as a contaminant to other mushroom cultures. Additionally, if over-incubated, they may fruit within containers, potentially threatening other cultures. 

Physically, oyster mushrooms are available in a variety of colors but are highly susceptible to damage during shipping and handling. They have a short functional lifespan of approximately five days, as they tend to spoil quickly, though some specific strains are more resistant to spoilage or temperature fluctuations than others.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-94b7579b911838ad215d22e4a1af54d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2c0c3389beaed103f9b9d331d49fd8c9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6330161a153f9e93f9660be64aa2f949&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e49dc22c5a2b0dc1af7b81f3d504b45f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c69b496654d5bdddbb3e95f39359af4e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dbf4d7e7600e83223b3cb9fc4c7377fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ac9c5eed9d523ae65975634b2284ac8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7663707174b73b4652d6d69af20a169b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ccb37e0cdb4a8da2abeb8e6f9a8e1962&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d79cc325ae91205a578658535a12a8d7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-211c569c81413a8e44e71f6820eda797&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0ca26f2f469a9af57a6edc3d86c628d6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee67735ee2d22fc01623c11e43940a78&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-69e12830990f7ea8d716253d971a2d83&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2b04e4252beb8b5fe756d225bcc2e8f9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4be6e62e9f709cb21a7f4bc14f814a83&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-422fbd40d9eaa3fb837b775984c912c2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-073e90e5615dba6d80d03e475296b301&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1634ffc0bb5eabb2ca88a56400372467&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffe4249d9b2a765fb568c54af6fb071b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d9231a797b87527809ee6f102a188204&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b7bfa69d0c84a828640655a9e1bb6d1a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a1569a67f674101fc6ab46edc7442941&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dc6f2bfb47ab5f2e3f74b6e3971de23d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e3a8d444ede80fcc9acce23623763b81&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2d9358e0c04b5f20ecd823fd8b55df7d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-526270b8a734dc850c078d50fae4f31c</data>
      <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 13}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 240}, {"level": 2, "cluster": 1110}, {"level": 3, "cluster": 1932}]</data>
