    <node id="&quot;JAPAN&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"LOCATION"</data>
      <data key="d1">Japan is a country located in East Asia and the Far East, encompassing the city of Nagoya and the Nara Experiment Station. It is recognized for its advanced agricultural practices, including the extensive use of night soil and composting, as well as being a primary example of effective soil erosion control through national policy and significant investment in the protection of rice fields. In the realm of international trade, Japan has imported U.S. beef, though it previously closed its doors to these imports due to mad cow disease, and it was the source of infested bees shipped to South America in the 1970s. Additionally, the country is noted as the place where the horse Statesman became a leading stallion.

Japan is a global leader in the cultivation, research, and commercialization of mushrooms. The country is a hub for the production of various species, including Shiitake (which are native, grown in greenhouses, and used to create products like wine and cookies), Enokitake (grown and exported), and Pholiota nameko, which is one of the most popular cultivated mushrooms and is commonly sold fresh. Other cultivated and researched fungi include Maitake (available in food markets and fruited from sterilized hardwood sawdust blocks), Kuritake, Shimeji (specifically Hypsizygus ulmarius, for which Japan has conducted significant research and patenting), and Yamabushitake, which is grown in tunnels.

The country also focuses on medicinal fungi; Ling Chi (Reishi) is held in near reverence and used as a flavor additive in beers and wines, with cultivation methods involving shaded logs or burying logs shallowly. Japan has also developed a $600 million industry centered around Agaricus blazei since 1995, refining strains in laboratories and cultivating them on sterilized substrates. Other notable fungi in Japan include G. frondosa (indigenous to the northeast), H. tessulatus, H. erinaceus, and H. sublateritium, the latter of which has seen pioneered cultivation via a specific outdoor inoculation method.

Technologically, Japanese cultivators employ advanced methods such as liquid spawn, hybrid bag methods, and bottle culture to accelerate growth. The country has also produced innovative derivatives from Agaricus brunnescens, such as breath fresheners and skin whiteners, and has approved lentinan as an anticancer drug. While Japan's weather patterns support the growth of the Black Poplar mushroom and the native P. citrinopileatus in the</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-5ad03c75085e62d4cf641ff00f56a870&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-95332b7697dfb1af6da09a5f6e7abd16&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-49fe442094e7ea7c8e57a86dad17be24&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2c0c3389beaed103f9b9d331d49fd8c9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30828eab432a849dd4130e8fbdeae05c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2960646489fe8d44fd3e37c8e77f3ee9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-26840155673e52eb4a69e8dc2ae438ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1f7009920cf127d7bc131b6a3452479d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-920c2b8a5eaf89205bbce9d1db91bfac&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-428e9d4590233f76ed9b454b23423b71&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-817bac06738b1392b1904426ad3a9798&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d27cfb98328aecc257da5d03f30c58f8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a81048c3992199bcae645ffac7f60bd3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4d1aec09332ff6a142a5567b1afdc379&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5aa9e3a4728dc666c9af9ca8874591f7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5481127c05291935ee74056db569bbd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-459d56574946408e6141e4469fce4745&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a64f9f1d0ae53b516c4de411cdfbaec4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-77998f10068e98f374d36ce3925f2d80&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d79cc325ae91205a578658535a12a8d7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b8363ae68571ed2f72d039837e5d11c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1fc5200861bfbf924cb02074bbc681c0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d467caefee31df754438e71491848a84&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5d6d051705a68de142e311b176db586&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a37142150e6090a50d92e1d1dabef730&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bb648e07d9441fbfe3bcee4498fbad8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-23851b7159c8512ddbf430fa390754ea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a71e41b29032039b5308b1f942743bd6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aea472dfebfe03f6968437bd2ffedd3d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e04f10386c4a39e5f50574580ea2171d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9deb5ea8a46288d76a6c05c601c4ab09&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-74cd72bbc78235ee2cc27e78214fb076&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2b5917356402ab1051d609ddf238bd22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-073e90e5615dba6d80d03e475296b301&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-04c7decd932543d02d71519d2eca78a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2f716dbb539b9e685559a05a8f9f5d54&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-064729886f339ac8895e2f324d6dd47e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d6710a74adf9d60f7c9121567c01098&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-01c91bc35d36c8498b9d61a9d491bf15&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a1569a67f674101fc6ab46edc7442941&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-325ed807d0495545fb84448a9a8a5863&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd4b544ed75741294a829a0b07b3bdf6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7c412d7b08105fd05e24e46b46f204fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64c2af8229ae517acd685a5341a4b3ed</data>
      <data key="d3">[{"level": 0, "cluster": 13}, {"level": 1, "cluster": 238}, {"level": 2, "cluster": 1094}, {"level": 3, "cluster": 1925}]</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;VARROA MITES&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"NORMAL_ENTITY"</data>
      <data key="d1">Varroa mites (*Varroa destructor*) are parasitic pests that infest honey bee colonies and pose a significant biological threat to the beekeeping industry. Introduced to the United States between 1984 and 1997, these mites attack honey bees by feeding on both the adult bees and the brood. This parasitic activity weakens the bees, compromises their immune systems, and can lead to deformed wings and parasitic mite syndrome. Furthermore, Varroa mites act as vectors that spread viruses and diseases throughout the hive, significantly impacting overwintering success and often leading to colony collapse and the loss of managed colonies.

Due to their impact on honey bee health and colony strength, Varroa mites are a primary focus of beekeeping management and genetic research. Beekeepers employ various control measures to reduce mite populations, including the use of screened bottom boards, drone combs, heat treatments, and methods such as trapping and zapping. While chemical treatments have been used, some mites have developed resistance to substances like amitraz. Consequently, there is an ongoing effort to develop resistant bee breeds and implement breeding strategies to ensure hive survival, which may include the introduction of new queens.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-68758adec1ce1aa5af3106fe56e7d365&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4f0965a7f888753898bdb3624830c9fd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-349ce4585e93cae4853e6da2e815c494&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-77dbdef00cf07d39bd84f0bbdd0936be&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-35f6eef0d2ff08b3a3b7b2c2e942edde&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dce07dbfe1cb37dbe56f8651f3f0f484&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-28ac9aabed87efda91436e0c9b5ff311&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-52fd5c92980e8846f221b5900200a76f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea68cfbbe3fc012d7e18af95936eb15e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50d84e8388b1b3835cf15d2729a7bded&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8745264800edf7a73b7e5b714dc70158&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-24285ce060eba50dee0f1aeb98d3fb26&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-073fb9290edc2d9768894a26e7788ead&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6287c81b3be930766b61c0ad32bf562f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b481828497c05f9df552dd81b644f543&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85263881590bbad34c8bcb9551c9a883&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d8286e6a8f25ded00034a7a287f09cf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-027615784640b56f822e7f3e6ed8eb5c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-622c003f3eb63c8fc5cb7dc192c0e76d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-09c586403b9fbc7d85f3084e5a000eb0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-09a1f67060248bded3f20fbcda85abc7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8201a44e8406b76d66752ef2bda352f5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fbe3628e117427ed1c35a791b026e9a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27c4227735c6f952b192f6d769deb712&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0c85b379d83c9f869435c42e6568dee9</data>
