    <node id="&quot;SUSTAINABLE AGRICULTURE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"INDUSTRY"</data>
      <data key="d1">Sustainable Agriculture is a broad, international movement and field of practice focused on developing farming methods that ensure long-term environmental health, economic viability, and social responsibility. As a concrete activity and field of study, it applies human, biological, and financial resources to create technology and social institutions capable of producing food while maintaining ecological integrity and system health.

This approach is often positioned as an alternative to industrial agriculture, drawing on principles of agrarianism to emphasize site-specific management, soil conservation, diversification, and ecological balance. It is described as a "whole-farm" approach aimed at reducing costs, increasing efficiency, and promoting resource diversity. Within the practice, there is an ongoing process of discovery regarding how various farm elements interact to remain environmentally sound. Specific methodologies, such as those championed by Gabe Brown, highlight the importance of soil management, while others, like those advocated by Kirschenmann, focus on utilizing smaller, labor-intensive practices to revitalize rural communities.

Beyond technical farming, Sustainable Agriculture is a subject of philosophical and social debate. It is viewed by some as a system that must incorporate social justice, living wages, and social equity to be truly sustainable. This holistic perspective extends to the development of local food systems and is supported by a loose network of organic producers, chefs, NGOs, and consumers. Whether applied to small or large-scale farms, Sustainable Agriculture seeks to provide for human needs and enhance environmental quality, ultimately aiming to change the world through responsible food production.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-12c1e39a2b39311bfd6f1c3c05dab313&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7dbc1c2ec064a792c47e97c74d0a8614&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a9a4c4a87611b19fd1b27e7ae81974b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2f4e1bfb463bad4d66c64f0be72baf51&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f3099841c9e59bebef5ddbb5acdb1e41&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1791122186a3d77110cdc1caf21599f0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-adc43a3f1c081aba3e143c209e36aef8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f0a5052e04a7b64a097459ad1bd8a957&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17984d082222b7d2f1526b0b77119804&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1bfda3cdec46f8ab5a386ecf15ae6edb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-416c88a4653bbd08275b4ad4b3f6d0d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c283b7bbdc3724574b4a465b7e4d29c6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-99e4abb0de7b0941ca1fc87ecc8fb4fd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6efabe1598e6b04e35168d3c337aa67b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2b6ff8589dcaca5214d3db25104a4fc2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85eaeec0849996dd41a39cefe3eb69e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ef577d840302c2f3d95d7cb5a8f77433&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-65953ac0df3c024d00056d147d289531&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3561dc31683327d8a6cc82dd5ad8b2b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0df07cda5b04335cf0c778b648161319&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c58f4785be38b80316bcdb547edbe48&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38d2ea190331fa57b51edf78d0352738&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-11c2b2726c61ccaf67be15464365be01&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-66bbc17b9fd4fb132a3a76abd2df0690&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b73ca7de046e79c23f93a20f80755db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6c15e66cc3147bff35c99fe3e874a056</data>
