    <node id="&quot;JOHN MILLER&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">John Miller is a professional commercial beekeeper, innovator, and advocate for honey bees who operates one of the largest honey-producing outfits in North Dakota while maintaining a home in California. A veteran of the industry and a descendant of N.E., Miller continues a family tradition through his work with Miller Honey Farms, though he frequently discusses the financial risks and economic hardships associated with modern apiculture. He manages a large-scale migratory operation, transporting thousands of hives between Idaho and California for the winter pollination circuit.

Described as a smart, engaged, and funny individual, John Miller is a father of four and a Mormon who is passionate about numbers and bee advocacy. He is a partner in both Inger and Honey Stinger, a collaborator of Zac Browning, and a board member of Project Apis mellifera. Known for his generosity and insights, Miller has gained significant media attention due to the Colony Collapse Disorder (CCD) crisis, which he attributes to "piss-poor management" (PPM).

As a steward of the species, Miller has spent years fighting parasites, pathogens, and the devastating effects of pesticides and varroa mites. He actively seeks solutions for bee health through advancements in genetics and purchases queen cells from Pat Heitkam to maintain his hives. An innovator in his field, he designed and developed a proprietary "nuking machine" to split hives more efficiently.

Beyond his commercial operations, John Miller grows Owari Satsuma mandarins on his property. His experiences in the industry are diverse, ranging from attending company anniversary parties for the Koehnens and conferences with Denis Anderson to dealing with the theft and subsequent recombination of his hives. Through his free-verse emails and historical accounts, Miller reflects on the unpredictability of beekeeping seasons, the profound loss of colonies, and the delicate balance between business management and environmental stewardship.</data>
      <data key="d2">chunk-de87ea9ee6a2918a9d0691e22c69a3bf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3c9fa8be9c480f16c9786fe42c4a409a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e60a1dc7299568504eb57c9d8e4201b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f71ef08ac19996ce0f766b5703b86c3d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fca26c7bdc4650cbef0a23f400c72ee8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cc6b42805d670ff5f1b75194ea815c5e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d268b118d865a964f0911adbcd24b46&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-88b98e2001c5ae7faad90b16ac45f0cf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-19b4571fdea2316371681c34c7f3af2b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8d1ac0226ffbeb324c79f5ab03c524d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-708bae7fc6ca7f9031d5fe8940db78f6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27c4a101237a6eb4eff648c0b0b5a16d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b6278bcba2e2dfe6f1202b147f87019&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7838234dc3bb876ced6fabea41222695&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dce07dbfe1cb37dbe56f8651f3f0f484&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-80e647f4fae9bf23b96a7c260839df22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9961d83fcb353d87b0c300b9b3408c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8cd73657f68ecf71018d63e394162ae2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa20c48947aea0538518c8462c9c2696&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-073fb9290edc2d9768894a26e7788ead&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-caa3c81818171fc49da384672e628346&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-855fe967816e81fd83bf1374e0f99957&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0984220078ce71973ba3b0fa10b37a2e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-001b3ca70f42e9cc2ce8671a72009988&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67d5af1111b2fbb8c1cd36ed46e7709b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e823f4271cc674d258a850b82bd8b70b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6deba29b237640ada67472e874a1f037&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c835bb690f30f5b9d4fe6eae38419d9f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-08ecb691c2f9f13d9852979644924e78&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e6a3d7885d5c7d0196e5f8b1d626686d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3e97945a272b68a7aa63d93a54b8c6ce&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6f025d035915467517ca424f6e1fea96</data>
