      <data key="d4">10.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"The Western honey bee is the only species of honey bee found in the Western Hemisphere."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-759e701020a9c2cf1b1fc48ccd55df97</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;HONEY BEE&quot;" target="&quot;NORTH AMERICA&quot;">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"The text discusses the number of bee species in North America and specifies that only one honey bee species exists there."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-759e701020a9c2cf1b1fc48ccd55df97</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;HONEY BEE&quot;" target="&quot;SOUTHEAST ASIA&quot;">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"Five of the six honey bee species worldwide are native to Southeast Asia."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-759e701020a9c2cf1b1fc48ccd55df97</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;HONEY BEE&quot;" target="&quot;WASPS&quot;">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"The text clarifies that bees are not wasps, though they are related and share similarities."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-759e701020a9c2cf1b1fc48ccd55df97</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;HONEY BEE&quot;" target="&quot;YELLOW JACKET&quot;">
      <data key="d4">12.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"The yellow jacket is a wasp that resembles the honey bee and is often mistaken for one."&lt;SEP&gt;"The yellow jacket resembles the honey bee but is only related at the suborder level."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-f2a11bfe1dc62c3ee867b5e29138914b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-759e701020a9c2cf1b1fc48ccd55df97</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;HONEY BEE&quot;" target="&quot;BUMBLEBEE&quot;">
      <data key="d4">15.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"The bumblebee is a closer relative of the honey bee and is a true bee, although not a honey bee."&lt;SEP&gt;"The bumblebee is a closer relative of the honey bee and is often confused with it."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-f2a11bfe1dc62c3ee867b5e29138914b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-759e701020a9c2cf1b1fc48ccd55df97</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;HONEY BEE&quot;" target="&quot;APIS MELLIFERA&quot;">
      <data key="d4">10.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"Apis mellifera is the scientific name for the honey bee (specifically the Western or European honey bee)."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-f2a11bfe1dc62c3ee867b5e29138914b</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;HONEY BEE&quot;" target="&quot;FIELD BEE&quot;">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"The honey bee's value as a pollinator is due to the constancy of the field bee's foraging habits."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-65de87b95deadb72c4827d5a3bd2f316</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;HONEY BEE&quot;" target="&quot;BEE BIOLOGY&quot;">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"Understanding the bee (biology) is essential to managing honey bee behavior and swarm control."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-64bd868f29e83effc96703b72e21cc18</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;HONEY BEE&quot;" target="&quot;NECTAR&quot;">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"Honey bees collect nectar from flowers to use as a source of energy and as the raw material for honey."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-64bd868f29e83effc96703b72e21cc18</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;HONEY BEE&quot;" target="&quot;POLLEN&quot;">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"Honey bees collect pollen for protein, which is necessary for the development of muscles, glands, and tissues."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-64bd868f29e83effc96703b72e21cc18</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;HONEY BEE&quot;" target="&quot;ROYAL JELLY&quot;">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"Royal jelly is the first food provided to honey bee brood during the larval stage."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-64bd868f29e83effc96703b72e21cc18</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;HONEY BEE&quot;" target="&quot;TRACHEAL MITES&quot;">
      <data key="d4">10.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"Tracheal mites are parasites that infest honey bees, living in their tracheae and shortening their lives."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-7a9a7dd90fa19da1cc0a36d8589ee8cd</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;HONEY BEE&quot;" target="&quot;K-WING&quot;">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"K-wing is a physical symptom/phenomenon observed in honey bees afflicted by tracheal mites."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-7a9a7dd90fa19da1cc0a36d8589ee8cd</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;HONEY BEE&quot;" target="&quot;MENTHOL&quot;">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"Menthol is applied to honey bee colonies to treat mites, although it can be disruptive to the colony and brood production."</data>
