    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;SUSTAINABILITY&quot;" target="&quot;AGRARIAN IDEALS&quot;">
      <data key="d4">6.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"Both sustainability and agrarian ideals are reconceptualized through the lens of embodied experience and the significance of specific places."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-3a9d0dea50021742bc52cffc66eae1b4</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;SUSTAINABILITY&quot;" target="&quot;AGRICULTURE&quot;">
      <data key="d4">17.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"Sustainability is defined as a goal for the entire system of producing and distributing agricultural commodities to minimize resource consumption."&lt;SEP&gt;"The text discusses how agriculture matters to sustainability and the philosophical rationale for its role in sustainable policies."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-1d63fd363bb5b8e772c6c0e59279b321&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0cd2b14e898ba9fd4a8dd4ab14491966</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;SUSTAINABILITY&quot;" target="&quot;THOMPSON&quot;">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"Thompson proposes a view of farming's contribution to common identity, which makes 'sustainability' a contentious word among mainstream agriculture."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-1d63fd363bb5b8e772c6c0e59279b321</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;SUSTAINABILITY&quot;" target="&quot;AIKEN&quot;">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"Aiken identifies sustainability as a moral goal for agriculture and attempts to account for the individual producer's obligation to practice it."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-0cd2b14e898ba9fd4a8dd4ab14491966</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;SUSTAINABILITY&quot;" target="&quot;ETHICAL ISSUES IN AGRICULTURE&quot;">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"In the essay 'Ethical Issues in Agriculture,' Aiken describes sustainability as an obligation owed directly to nature."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-0cd2b14e898ba9fd4a8dd4ab14491966</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;SUSTAINABILITY&quot;" target="&quot;ROBERT SOLOW&quot;">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"Robert Solow's work is the basis for the 'weak sustainability' approach, which argues that reproduced capital can substitute for natural resources."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-a98ffdda5b456b304e6585d043b124d6</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;SUSTAINABILITY&quot;" target="&quot;BRUNDTLAND REPORT&quot;">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"The Brundtland Report defines a commitment to sustain a general level of welfare for future generations, a key component of the sustainability debate."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-a98ffdda5b456b304e6585d043b124d6</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;SUSTAINABILITY&quot;" target="&quot;BRYAN NORTON&quot;">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"Bryan Norton summarized the core disagreement between weak sustainability (economic growth) and strong sustainability (maintaining 'stuff')."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-a98ffdda5b456b304e6585d043b124d6</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;SUSTAINABILITY&quot;" target="&quot;BIOTECHNOLOGIES&quot;">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"Biotechnologies are discussed as potential substitutes for natural resource maintenance (like soil fertility), central to the debate on sustainability."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-a98ffdda5b456b304e6585d043b124d6</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;SUSTAINABILITY&quot;" target="&quot;RESOURCE SUFFICIENCY&quot;">
      <data key="d4">17.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"Resource sufficiency is one of the general approaches used to specify and idealize sustainability."&lt;SEP&gt;"The author identifies resource sufficiency as one of the leading ideas used to understand and match up with notions of sustainability."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-5004d71f44c231b6939419f18c324a3b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6efabe1598e6b04e35168d3c337aa67b</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;SUSTAINABILITY&quot;" target="&quot;FUNCTIONAL INTEGRITY&quot;">
      <data key="d4">17.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"Functional integrity is another general approach to sustainability, focusing on the mechanisms that allow systems to regenerate."&lt;SEP&gt;"The author presents functional integrity as a leading idea and a promising framework for pursuing sustainability."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-5004d71f44c231b6939419f18c324a3b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6efabe1598e6b04e35168d3c337aa67b</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
