    <edge source="&quot;AGRARIAN DREAMS: THE PARADOX OF ORGANIC FARMING IN CALIFORNIA&quot;" target="&quot;CALIFORNIA&quot;">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"The book analyzes the paradoxes of organic farming specifically within the state of California."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-2bc219a87c7ecb22911c9b9083bd392f</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;LIBERTARIANS&quot;" target="&quot;JEFFERSONIAN DEMOCRACY&quot;">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"Jeffersonian democracy shares liberal political ideals that are debated among libertarians."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-1c613cbad49e71f1cd416e26e34150f0</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;LABOR MOVEMENT&quot;" target="&quot;INDUSTRIAL AGRICULTURE&quot;">
      <data key="d4">6.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"Industrial agriculture relies on motorized equipment, which has decreased the importance and influence of the labor movement in farm production."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-1e3da01f719a88fbedfd67f21916a1c0</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;LABOR MOVEMENT&quot;" target="&quot;SOCIOLOGISTS&quot;">
      <data key="d4">6.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"Sociologists study the labor movement as a model of a successful classic social movement."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-7e2ee6e34caebeb195612046b45062fe</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;LABOR MOVEMENT&quot;" target="&quot;NEW SOCIAL MOVEMENT THEORY&quot;">
      <data key="d4">6.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"The labor movement is cited as an example of a 'classic social movement' to be contrasted with the 'new social movement theory'."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-11c2b2726c61ccaf67be15464365be01</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;LABOR MOVEMENT&quot;" target="&quot;SOCIAL MOVEMENT THEORISTS&quot;">
      <data key="d4">6.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"The labor movement is cited as an example of the types of social movements that achieve change, relating back to the social movement view."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-c7cbdcc7923d3d20d9cd83a4cae784b4</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;NEOLIBERALISM&quot;" target="&quot;INDUSTRIAL AGRICULTURE&quot;">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"By 1980, industrial agriculture became the preferred view of those committed to neoliberal visions of limited government and free markets."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-1e3da01f719a88fbedfd67f21916a1c0</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;CANADIANS&quot;" target="&quot;INDUSTRIAL AGRICULTURE&quot;">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"Industrial agriculture has allowed Canadians to spend far less of their personal income on food than in previous history."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-1e3da01f719a88fbedfd67f21916a1c0</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;ENVIRONMENTAL MOVEMENT&quot;" target="&quot;INDUSTRIAL AGRICULTURE&quot;">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"The environmental movement stresses the dark side and environmental costs of industrial agriculture's methods."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-1e3da01f719a88fbedfd67f21916a1c0</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;NORTH AMERICAN AGRIBUSINESS&quot;" target="&quot;INDUSTRIAL AGRICULTURE&quot;">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"North American agribusiness is increasingly adopting 'organic industrial agriculture' to meet market demands within the industrial food system."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-1e3da01f719a88fbedfd67f21916a1c0</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;JEFFERSONIAN VISION&quot;" target="&quot;INDUSTRIAL AGRICULTURE&quot;">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"The Jeffersonian vision of the citizen-farmer is presented as an intellectual and political alternative to the industrial vision of agriculture."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-1e3da01f719a88fbedfd67f21916a1c0</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;MARXISM&quot;" target="&quot;INDUSTRIAL TECHNOLOGY&quot;">
      <data key="d4">5.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"The text contrasts Marxism's critique of private property with an industrial philosophy of agriculture that some leftists (who endorsed industrial technology) supported."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-2b561afe8a3fd467795e6d58a0118448</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;INDUSTRIAL TECHNOLOGY&quot;" target="&quot;TECHNOLOGICAL TREADMILL&quot;">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"The acceptance of technical efficiency in absolute terms rationalizes the technological treadmill, which drives small farms out of business."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-2b561afe8a3fd467795e6d58a0118448</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;NORTH AMERICAN HEARTLAND&quot;" target="&quot;PRINCIPLE OF AGRARIAN LOCALISM&quot;">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"The self-reliant farmsteads and communal needs of the North American heartland support the development of the principle of agrarian localism."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-2b561afe8a3fd467795e6d58a0118448</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;GREAT AGRARIAN GOAL&quot;" target="&quot;HENRY DAVID THOREAU&quot;">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"Thoreau's quest for a simple life to open space for self-realization serves as a philosophical precedent for the Great Agrarian Goal."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-2b561afe8a3fd467795e6d58a0118448</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;GREAT AGRARIAN GOAL&quot;" target="&quot;RALPH WALDO EMERSON&quot;">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"Emerson's focus on self-realization serves as a philosophical precedent for the Great Agrarian Goal."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-2b561afe8a3fd467795e6d58a0118448</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;HENRY DAVID THOREAU&quot;" target="&quot;AMERICAN TRANSCENDENTALISTS&quot;">
      <data key="d4">10.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"Henry David Thoreau is identified as one of the American transcendentalists."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-2b561afe8a3fd467795e6d58a0118448</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;RALPH WALDO EMERSON&quot;" target="&quot;AMERICAN TRANSCENDENTALISTS&quot;">
      <data key="d4">10.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"Ralph Waldo Emerson is identified as one of the American transcendentalists."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-2b561afe8a3fd467795e6d58a0118448</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;THOREAU&quot;" target="&quot;EMERSON&quot;">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"Both are associated with transcendentalism in the context of their thinking on spiritual consciousness."&lt;SEP&gt;"Both were committed to a philosophical program of interpreting spirituality and society in naturalistic terms and shared a critique of industrialization."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-3330698527e14dc575dc6a2e0e2a7c9d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-609aab6a070f0cc5d446cf17636cca13</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;THOREAU&quot;" target="&quot;THE TRANSCENDENTALISTS&quot;">
      <data key="d4">10.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"Thoreau was part of the transcendentalist movement, sharing their belief that industrial ways of life were unsuitable for human natural capacities."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-3330698527e14dc575dc6a2e0e2a7c9d</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;THOREAU&quot;" target="&quot;WALDEN&quot;">
      <data key="d4">10.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"Thoreau wrote 'Walden', in which he discusses his desire to engage with the flow of embodied experience."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-3330698527e14dc575dc6a2e0e2a7c9d</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;THOREAU&quot;" target="&quot;DOUGLAS ANDERSON&quot;">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"Douglas Anderson identifies and labels Thoreau's specific philosophy regarding agriculture as 'wild farming'."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-3330698527e14dc575dc6a2e0e2a7c9d</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;THOREAU&quot;" target="&quot;WILD FARMING&quot;">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"Thoreau advocated for a philosophy of 'wild farming' that emphasizes attentiveness to a partially civilized wild."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-3330698527e14dc575dc6a2e0e2a7c9d</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;THOREAU&quot;" target="&quot;T. H. GREEN&quot;">
      <data key="d4">5.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"Green's view of transcendent spiritual consciousness is suggested to be similar to the transcendentalism of Thoreau."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-609aab6a070f0cc5d446cf17636cca13</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;THOREAU&quot;" target="&quot;F. H. BRADLEY&quot;">
      <data key="d4">5.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"Bradley's view of transcendent spiritual consciousness is suggested to be similar to the transcendentalism of Thoreau."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-609aab6a070f0cc5d446cf17636cca13</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;THOREAU&quot;" target="&quot;JOHN JAMES AUDUBON&quot;">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"Both are identified as early environmentalists shaping a new ecological awareness and environmental ethic."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-c1d159b4784f26ff000ba4dfed046754</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;EMERSON&quot;" target="&quot;THE TRANSCENDENTALISTS&quot;">
      <data key="d4">10.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"Emerson was a key figure among the transcendentalists, promoting the idea of the naturally virtuous life."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-3330698527e14dc575dc6a2e0e2a7c9d</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;EMERSON&quot;" target="&quot;GERMAN IDEALISM&quot;">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"Emerson was deeply influenced by German idealism in his early thought, particularly regarding nature and poetic acts."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-3330698527e14dc575dc6a2e0e2a7c9d</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;EMERSON&quot;" target="&quot;HEGEL&quot;">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"Emerson and Hegel shared views on poetry as a way to realize the potential for creativity and spirituality."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-3330698527e14dc575dc6a2e0e2a7c9d</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;EMERSON&quot;" target="&quot;T. H. GREEN&quot;">
      <data key="d4">5.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"Green's view of transcendent spiritual consciousness is suggested to be similar to the transcendentalism of Emerson."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-609aab6a070f0cc5d446cf17636cca13</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;EMERSON&quot;" target="&quot;F. H. BRADLEY&quot;">
      <data key="d4">5.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"Bradley's view of transcendent spiritual consciousness is suggested to be similar to the transcendentalism of Emerson."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-609aab6a070f0cc5d446cf17636cca13</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;HEGEL&quot;" target="&quot;IMMANUEL KANT&quot;">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"Hegel took issue with Kant's claims regarding the foundation of duties to autonomous beings based on rational certainty."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-a52b953a69d1e5300dc42fea549624cc</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;HEGEL&quot;" target="&quot;T. H. GREEN&quot;">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"T. H. Green is identified as a later Hegelian who developed ethical systems based on Hegel's influence."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-a52b953a69d1e5300dc42fea549624cc</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;HEGEL&quot;" target="&quot;F. H. BRADLEY&quot;">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"F. H. Bradley is identified as a later Hegelian who developed ethical systems based on Hegel's influence."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-a52b953a69d1e5300dc42fea549624cc</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;HEGEL&quot;" target="&quot;MASTER-SLAVE DIALECTIC&quot;">
      <data key="d4">10.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"Hegel developed the master-slave dialectic to describe the sequence of oppositions and self-recognition in consciousness."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-fa6c175f37ce7089fa657f33595c666c</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;HEGEL&quot;" target="&quot;KARL MARX&quot;">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"Karl Marx developed a version of Hegel's dialectic, focusing on material confrontations between social classes."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-fa6c175f37ce7089fa657f33595c666c</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;HEGEL&quot;" target="&quot;FRIEDRICH ENGELS&quot;">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"Friedrich Engels, along with Marx, developed a version of Hegel's dialectic applied to social classes."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-fa6c175f37ce7089fa657f33595c666c</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;HEGEL&quot;" target="&quot;THE CHURCH&quot;">
      <data key="d4">6.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"Hegel's framework suggests consciousness recognizes its social being through embodiment in institutions like the church."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-fa6c175f37ce7089fa657f33595c666c</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
