    <edge source="&quot;EFFICIENCY&quot;" target="&quot;AGRICULTURAL ECONOMISTS&quot;">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"Agricultural economists promote a more comprehensive approach to efficiency that includes long-term costs and benefits to parties outside the farm."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-c08392ee1c44f7a4ba02412343bf13d1</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;AGRICULTURAL ECONOMISTS&quot;" target="&quot;INDUSTRIAL AGRICULTURE&quot;">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"Agricultural economists use a richer notion of efficiency to test the belief that the industrialization of food production is truly efficient."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-c08392ee1c44f7a4ba02412343bf13d1</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;INDUSTRIALIZATION&quot;" target="&quot;SOCIAL EFFICIENCY&quot;">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"The text uses industrialization of food production as a case study to test and define the concept of social efficiency, weighing producer costs against consumer benefits."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-4e994cbfe6a9e41aacdea8c3494eaa2e</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;INDUSTRIALIZATION&quot;" target="&quot;RUTH COWAN&quot;">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"Ruth Cowan analyzes household self-reliance and gender roles as they existed before industrialization."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-cd6cef65b73cbf090d0f46d890908a11</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;INDUSTRIALIZATION&quot;" target="&quot;JEFFERSON&quot;">
      <data key="d4">6.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"Jefferson's era of household self-reliance is contrasted with the subsequent period of industrialization described by Cowan."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-cd6cef65b73cbf090d0f46d890908a11</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;SOCIAL EFFICIENCY&quot;" target="&quot;UTILITARIAN PHILOSOPHERS&quot;">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"Utilitarian philosophers provided the conceptual framework for social efficiency through the expression 'the greatest good for the greatest number'."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-4e994cbfe6a9e41aacdea8c3494eaa2e</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;SOCIAL EFFICIENCY&quot;" target="&quot;FREE MARKETS&quot;">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"The argument for social efficiency is often reinterpreted as an endorsement of unregulated markets and free markets."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-4e994cbfe6a9e41aacdea8c3494eaa2e</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;SOCIAL EFFICIENCY&quot;" target="&quot;PROPERTY RIGHTS&quot;">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"Social efficiency is interdependently defined with property rights; measures of efficiency presume a given set of property rules."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-b617c920b621d8c4334557c2c41bce8a</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;ARIEL&quot;" target="&quot;AKBAR&quot;">
      <data key="d4">14.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"Ariel and Akbar are used as examples of individuals engaging in trades, which are dependent on the rules of property rights."&lt;SEP&gt;"Ariel and Akbar are used as hypothetical examples of individuals engaging in a trade of resources to illustrate how social efficiency is measured by willingness to pay."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-b617c920b621d8c4334557c2c41bce8a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4e994cbfe6a9e41aacdea8c3494eaa2e</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;ARIEL&quot;" target="&quot;FREE MARKETS&quot;">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"The interaction between Ariel and Akbar is used to demonstrate the logic that defends free markets based on voluntary exchange."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-4e994cbfe6a9e41aacdea8c3494eaa2e</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;ARIEL&quot;" target="&quot;PROPERTY RIGHTS&quot;">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"Ariel's ability to trade and her legal constraints regarding Akbar's resources are determined by the enforcement of property rights."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-b617c920b621d8c4334557c2c41bce8a</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;AKBAR&quot;" target="&quot;FREE MARKETS&quot;">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"The interaction between Ariel and Akbar is used to demonstrate the logic that defends free markets based on voluntary exchange."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-4e994cbfe6a9e41aacdea8c3494eaa2e</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;AKBAR&quot;" target="&quot;PROPERTY RIGHTS&quot;">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"Akbar's ownership and compensation for resources are governed by the system of property rights."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-b617c920b621d8c4334557c2c41bce8a</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;THE SPIRIT OF THE SOIL&quot;" target="&quot;FREE MARKETS&quot;">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"The Spirit of the Soil is a work where the author develops a critique of the view that justifies free markets through the lens of social efficiency."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-4e994cbfe6a9e41aacdea8c3494eaa2e</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
