      <data key="d6">chunk-ddd79b341dfcb637bbc86f0265484513</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;RESOURCE SUFFICIENCY&quot;" target="&quot;FUNCTIONAL INTEGRITY&quot;">
      <data key="d4">14.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"The author compares these two as leading ideas that differ based on the types of value judgments informing each approach."&lt;SEP&gt;"The text compares and contrasts these two approaches, noting they are normatively different but often lead to similar empirical questions."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-5004d71f44c231b6939419f18c324a3b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6efabe1598e6b04e35168d3c337aa67b</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;RESOURCE SUFFICIENCY&quot;" target="&quot;JOHN RAWLS&quot;">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"John Rawls's idea of primary goods is used to articulate how the resource sufficiency approach can be made compatible with rights- or autonomy-based ethics."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-5004d71f44c231b6939419f18c324a3b</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;RESOURCE SUFFICIENCY&quot;" target="&quot;AMARTYA SEN&quot;">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"Amartya Sen's idea of capabilities is used to articulate how the resource sufficiency approach can be made compatible with rights- or autonomy-based ethics."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-5004d71f44c231b6939419f18c324a3b</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
