      <data key="d4">5.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"The Africanized honey bee is described as a warm-weather cousin to the European honey bee, contrasting in their ability to survive cold winters."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-027615784640b56f822e7f3e6ed8eb5c</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;EUROPEAN HONEY BEE&quot;" target="&quot;VARROA MITES&quot;">
      <data key="d4">37.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"European honey bees are weakened by varroa mites, which feed on them and spread diseases, challenging their winter survival."&lt;SEP&gt;"Varroa mites are identified as pests of the European honey bee."&lt;SEP&gt;"Varroa mites are parasites that affect the European honey bee, and research focuses on their ability to coexist."&lt;SEP&gt;"Varroa mites parasitize European honey bee brood, weakening their immune systems and potentially leading to colony collapse."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-5fbe3628e117427ed1c35a791b026e9a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8201a44e8406b76d66752ef2bda352f5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-027615784640b56f822e7f3e6ed8eb5c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-28ac9aabed87efda91436e0c9b5ff311</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;EUROPEAN HONEY BEE&quot;" target="&quot;NUCLEUS COLONY&quot;">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"Nucleus colonies are created by dividing existing European honey bee hives to build bee stock acclimated to a specific locale."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-027615784640b56f822e7f3e6ed8eb5c</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
