      <data key="d5">"Staircase cultivation was practiced by the ancient Peruvians to grow crops in upland areas."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-cbe735eff1ddb5f51823657c9cb6e03f</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;PERUVIANS&quot;" target="&quot;SOUTH AMERICAN VALLEYS&quot;">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"The Peruvians lived in the South American valleys but were driven to upland areas because they could not remove dense forest growths."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-cbe735eff1ddb5f51823657c9cb6e03f</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;INCA NATION&quot;" target="&quot;ANCIENT PERUVIANS&quot;">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"The text refers to the Inca nation and the ancient Peruvians in the context of the same civilization's agricultural achievements (stone terraces and aqueducts)."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-1101930a67ed83a3fac551ddd493eeef</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;GILGIT AGENCY&quot;" target="&quot;MCCARRISON&quot;">
      <data key="d4">10.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"McCarrison served as the Medical Officer to the Gilgit Agency."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-ac5c0d8327eb6e62733fe07d24f28c17</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;AFRICAN CONTINENT&quot;" target="&quot;FAR EAST&quot;">
      <data key="d4">6.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"The text notes that the African continent never came into contact with the agricultural peoples of the Far East to revise its cultivation systems."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-1101930a67ed83a3fac551ddd493eeef</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;FAR EAST&quot;" target="&quot;AGARICUS BLAZEI&quot;">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"Cultivation centers for A. blazei are being built throughout the Far East."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-d27cfb98328aecc257da5d03f30c58f8</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;FAR EAST&quot;" target="&quot;KING&quot;">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"King conducted estimations of nutrient cycling and waste usage across the Far East."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-04c7decd932543d02d71519d2eca78a1</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;EASTERN ASIA&quot;" target="&quot;CHINA&quot;">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"The text discusses the successful agricultural solutions in eastern Asia, specifically detailing the methods used in China."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-1101930a67ed83a3fac551ddd493eeef</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;COMPOST&quot;" target="&quot;CHINA&quot;">
      <data key="d4">10.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"The Chinese peasant utilizes the device of making compost to supply fields with humus and maintain soil fertility."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-1101930a67ed83a3fac551ddd493eeef</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;COMPOST&quot;" target="&quot;S. D. TIMSON&quot;">
      <data key="d4">10.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"S. D. Timson observed, tested, and encouraged farmers to use compost to prevent eelworm attacks."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-32652f7d3d3157d503e192ffd61dd2fc</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;COMPOST&quot;" target="&quot;E. C. HOLMES&quot;">
      <data key="d4">10.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"E. C. Holmes used compost to successfully eradicate eelworm from his vegetable gardens."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-32652f7d3d3157d503e192ffd61dd2fc</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;COMPOST&quot;" target="&quot;WITCHWEED DEMONSTRATION FARM&quot;">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"The Witchweed Demonstration Farm provided a striking example where compost cleared a severe eelworm infestation."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-32652f7d3d3157d503e192ffd61dd2fc</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;COMPOST&quot;" target="&quot;ARTIFICIAL MANURES&quot;">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"The text contrasts the failure of artificial manures (which lead to soil degradation) with the success of compost in restoring soil health."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-32652f7d3d3157d503e192ffd61dd2fc</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;COMPOST&quot;" target="&quot;PROTEIN&quot;">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"Growing affected plants in freshly prepared compost replaces imperfectly synthesized protein with normal protein."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-f9a35a75b0bc3a557b68f4dc4d5219ba</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;COMPOST&quot;" target="&quot;NEW ZEALAND HUMIC COMPOST CLUB&quot;">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"The Club publishes a magazine titled 'Compost' and promotes the use of humus/compost for soil fertilization."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-dc52f86574e1bfc1754f84c90ed93d24</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;COMPOST&quot;" target="&quot;BIOLOGICAL COMMUNITY&quot;">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"Compost (referred to by the neighbor as mulch) is used to build the biological community of the soil to increase productivity."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-1fb4383345b96028850ce009197a2ceb</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;COMPOST&quot;" target="&quot;CHEMICAL FERTILIZER&quot;">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"The author references scientists who prove that compost is a better alternative than chemical fertilizer."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-f4abff4036ca3ecfbb4d0e2600cc253d</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;WESTERN CIVILIZATION&quot;" target="&quot;CAPITALISTIC SOCIETY&quot;">
      <data key="d4">6.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"The text links the profit-driven design of a capitalistic society to the industrialized food system that Western civilization relies upon."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-6aa4dbd1369009e95184a34301a90d2f</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;WESTERN CIVILIZATION&quot;" target="&quot;INDUSTRIAL AGRICULTURE&quot;">
      <data key="d4">6.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"Western civilization relies on a food system that is threatened by the collapse of pollinators, which is linked to practices like industrial agriculture."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-0b56d1daf41a7135af792e5f17f10061</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;WESTERN CIVILIZATION&quot;" target="&quot;US DOLLAR&quot;">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"The economic system of Western civilization is designed around the current monetary system (US dollar) and is predicted to collapse."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-2ab141b6aa8655dd452f4b60acea75bc</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;GREEK NATION&quot;" target="&quot;MALARIA&quot;">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"A theory suggests that the collapse of the Greek nation in the 4th and 3rd centuries B.C. was due to the spread of malaria."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-7197b1327e60ebc0c4ee2f3ed2bd7e13</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;ROME&quot;" target="&quot;ALEXANDRIA&quot;">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"Rome, Alexandria, and Antioch were all great cities that the Romans had to feed from external sources."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-7197b1327e60ebc0c4ee2f3ed2bd7e13</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;ROME&quot;" target="&quot;AGRICULTURE&quot;">
      <data key="d4">6.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"Rome is cited as an example of a society that experienced decline due to the loss of its agricultural population."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-571b3fa101d4bb8e213e6691eda956ff</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;ROME&quot;" target="&quot;FOOD AND AGRICULTURE ORGANIZATION (FAO) OF THE UNITED NATIONS&quot;">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"The FAO of the United Nations is located in or published the work from Rome."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-f8bc16c9da1f7a4802773914f2b68bd6</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;ROME&quot;" target="&quot;CARTHAGE&quot;">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"Rome sacked Carthage and later used its land for farming after destroying the city."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-14eaa33165675c404b6f3cd5708028e8</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;ROME&quot;" target="&quot;CENTRAL ITALY&quot;">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"Rome's shift to large plantations and slave labor triggered serious soil erosion across central Italy."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-14eaa33165675c404b6f3cd5708028e8</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;ROME&quot;" target="&quot;OSTIA&quot;">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"Sedimentation caused by erosion in the Roman hinterland stranded Rome's ancient port of Ostia inland."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-14eaa33165675c404b6f3cd5708028e8</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;ROME&quot;" target="&quot;NORTH AFRICA&quot;">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"North African farms were essential for feeding Rome, and Roman farmers eventually worked the land there."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-14eaa33165675c404b6f3cd5708028e8</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;ROME&quot;" target="&quot;SYRIA&quot;">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"Syrian hills exported grain to feed Rome, but are now degraded rocky slopes."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-14eaa33165675c404b6f3cd5708028e8</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;ROME&quot;" target="&quot;EASTER ISLAND&quot;">
      <data key="d4">5.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"Both are listed as regions where versions of the story of soil degradation and civilization collapse played out."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-187f47c8b8e6814f319c165475064ae6</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;ROME&quot;" target="&quot;CHINA&quot;">
      <data key="d4">5.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"Both are listed as regions where versions of the story of soil degradation and civilization collapse played out."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-187f47c8b8e6814f319c165475064ae6</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;ROME&quot;" target="&quot;FOOD AND AGRICULTURE ORGANIZATION OF THE UNITED NATIONS (FAO)&quot;">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"The FAO is located in or the document was published from Rome."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-a8b6ee73bf4aad542645163d48412ef2</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;LATIFUNDIUM&quot;" target="&quot;PASTORAL INDUSTRY&quot;">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"The latifundium system eventually led to the final phase where tillage was abandoned in favor of the cheaper pastoral industry."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-7197b1327e60ebc0c4ee2f3ed2bd7e13</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;LATIFUNDIUM&quot;" target="&quot;CICERO&quot;">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"Cicero was a writer who deeply deplored the disastrous change toward latifundia and the loss of small farms."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-7197b1327e60ebc0c4ee2f3ed2bd7e13</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;MIXED HUSBANDRY&quot;" target="&quot;EUROPEAN MANURE HEAP&quot;">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"Mixed husbandry depended on the production of manure, which was collected and stored using the European manure heap."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-86a7f4aa7178f919427c0954ff8040e8</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;MIXED HUSBANDRY&quot;" target="&quot;FALLOW&quot;">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"The use of the fallow was a third principle in conserving fertility within the system of mixed husbandry."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-86a7f4aa7178f919427c0954ff8040e8</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;MIXED HUSBANDRY&quot;" target="&quot;AGRICULTURE&quot;">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"The author argues that the abandonment of mixed husbandry is the most fatal error committed by modern agriculture."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-f327dcda762579e3e8ff20376026fc06</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;CESSPIT&quot;" target="&quot;WATER-BORNE SEWAGE&quot;">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"Water-borne sewage was invented and rapidly replaced the less successful use of the cesspit."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-86a7f4aa7178f919427c0954ff8040e8</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;WATER-BORNE SEWAGE&quot;" target="&quot;SANITARY SCIENCE&quot;">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"Water-borne sewage and the disposal of refuse were driven by the dictates of the new sanitary science."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-86a7f4aa7178f919427c0954ff8040e8</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;BASIN SYSTEM OF IRRIGATION&quot;" target="&quot;PERENNIAL IRRIGATION&quot;">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"The basin system of irrigation in Egypt has recently been replaced by perennial irrigation to allow for cotton growth."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-86a7f4aa7178f919427c0954ff8040e8</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;ROMAN OCCUPATION&quot;" target="&quot;GREAT BRITAIN&quot;">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"During the Roman occupation, the Romans developed Great Britain by creating agricultural units."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-86a7f4aa7178f919427c0954ff8040e8</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;GAUL&quot;" target="&quot;BELGIC TRIBES&quot;">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"The Belgic tribes exported considerable quantities of wheat to Gaul."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-b7059817578993eac630dd1b076722a1</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;GAUL&quot;" target="&quot;MOULD BOARD PLOUGH&quot;">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"The first recorded references to the mould board plough are located in Gaul."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-bc828547e31ad0722ddb6c9ae3f92d30</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;THE CONTINENT&quot;" target="&quot;EMPEROR JULIAN&quot;">
      <data key="d4">6.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"Emperor Julian's administration facilitated the movement of 800 wheat ships from Britain to the Continent."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-b7059817578993eac630dd1b076722a1</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;SAXON INVADERS&quot;" target="&quot;WESER&quot;">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"The settlers (Saxons) previously inhabited the belts of land around the Weser."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-b7059817578993eac630dd1b076722a1</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;SAXON INVADERS&quot;" target="&quot;ELBE&quot;">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"The settlers (Saxons) previously inhabited the belts of land around the Elbe."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-b7059817578993eac630dd1b076722a1</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;ANGLO-SAXON SOCIETY&quot;" target="&quot;ENGLAND&quot;">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"The formation of the village community in early Anglo-Saxon society was a key origin of the open-field husbandry in England."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-bc828547e31ad0722ddb6c9ae3f92d30</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;NORTH-WEST INDIA&quot;" target="&quot;INDIA&quot;">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"North-west India is identified as one of the two most important cotton growing areas in India."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-635531b84bb4fbfde1eef86a36c0896a</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;NORTH-WEST INDIA&quot;" target="&quot;INDO-GANGETIC PLAIN&quot;">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"The alluvium of north-west India consists of deposits left by the rivers of the Indo-Gangetic plain."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-635531b84bb4fbfde1eef86a36c0896a</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;NORTH-WEST INDIA&quot;" target="&quot;PUNJAB&quot;">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"The Punjab is a specific region within north-west India where cotton ripening periods are unduly prolonged."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-635531b84bb4fbfde1eef86a36c0896a</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;SAXONS&quot;" target="&quot;ENGLAND&quot;">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"The Saxons brought lands into cultivation in England and used soil-inverting ploughs."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-bc828547e31ad0722ddb6c9ae3f92d30</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;SAXONS&quot;" target="&quot;HEAVY PLOUGH&quot;">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"Saxons from the earliest times used a soil-inverting plough (heavy plough) which affected soil humus."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-bc828547e31ad0722ddb6c9ae3f92d30</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;GERMANIC PEOPLE&quot;" target="&quot;MOULD BOARD PLOUGH&quot;">
      <data key="d4">6.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"Some authorities suggest the Germanic people borrowed the mould board plough from the Slavs."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-bc828547e31ad0722ddb6c9ae3f92d30</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;GERMANIC PEOPLE&quot;" target="&quot;SLAVS&quot;">
      <data key="d4">5.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"The Germanic people may have borrowed the mould board plough from the Slavs."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-bc828547e31ad0722ddb6c9ae3f92d30</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;NORTH EUROPE&quot;" target="&quot;MOULD BOARD PLOUGH&quot;">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"The evolution of the big plough (mould board) was driven by the heavy, moist soils of north Europe."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-bc828547e31ad0722ddb6c9ae3f92d30</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;CELTS&quot;" target="&quot;HEAVY PLOUGH&quot;">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"The Celts used light scratch ploughs, which were useless on the heavy soils that the heavy mould board plough could handle."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-bc828547e31ad0722ddb6c9ae3f92d30</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;HEAVY PLOUGH&quot;" target="&quot;MOULD BOARD PLOUGH&quot;">
      <data key="d4">10.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"The heavy plough and the mould board plough are referred to as the same soil-inverting tool used in medieval tillage."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-bc828547e31ad0722ddb6c9ae3f92d30</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;MOULD BOARD PLOUGH&quot;" target="&quot;ENGLAND&quot;">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"The mould board plough determined the lay-out of open fields in England and allowed the cropped area to be extended."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-bc828547e31ad0722ddb6c9ae3f92d30</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;HEAVY MOULD BOARD PLOUGH&quot;" target="&quot;SCRATCH PLOUGH&quot;">
      <data key="d4">6.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"The heavy mould board plough is contrasted with the light scratch plough, offering higher penetrating power for heavier soils."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-0a20dca5473a46eeac4310d35a490fdf</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;HEAVY MOULD BOARD PLOUGH&quot;" target="&quot;ENGLAND&quot;">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"The use of the heavy mould board plough allowed the cropped area in England to be greatly extended by cultivating heavy soils."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-0a20dca5473a46eeac4310d35a490fdf</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;HEAVY MOULD BOARD PLOUGH&quot;" target="&quot;OPEN-FIELD SYSTEM&quot;">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">"The heavy mould board plough was used to cultivate the strips of the open-field system, but its soil-inverting nature contributed to the system's eventual failure by wearing out the land."</data>
      <data key="d6">chunk-0a20dca5473a46eeac4310d35a490fdf</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
    </edge>
